= Erlenmeyer flask
{c}
{parent=Chemistry}

= Conical flask
{synonym}

A <conical flask> has a narrow neck and a wider body that permits swirling a <chemical solution>, as in a <redox titration>.

= Radical
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Free radical
{synonym}

A <radical (chemistry)> has one or more unpaired <electrons>. In a simple <atomic orbital> or <molecular orbital> occupancy picture, an unpaired <electron> occupies a spatial orbital without an opposite-<spin> partner in that orbital. A dot such as $\mathrm H^{\bullet}$ marks this electronic property; it does not mean a missing <electron> or positive <electric charge>. <Free radicals> can be neutral or charged. Terminology also has a boundary: metal <ions> exhibiting <paramagnetism> are not normally called <free radicals> just because they have unpaired <electrons>.<Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry (IUPAC Recommendations 1994): R entries by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry, IUPAC Recommendations 1994: radical (or free radical), opening definition and metal-ion qualification]{ref}

Compare three species containing the same single <proton>. The <hydrogen atom> $\mathrm H^{\bullet}$ has one <electron> in its $1s$ <atomic orbital>, and is a neutral <radical (chemistry)>. The <hydrogen> <cation> $\mathrm H^+$ has no <electrons>, so no unpaired <electron>. The <hydrogen anion> $\mathrm H^-$ has two <electrons> paired in its $1s$ <atomic orbital>. It is charged but is not a <radical (chemistry)> in its <ground state>. With $N_p$ <protons>, $N_e$ <electrons>, and positive <elementary charge> $e$, the independent <electric charge> count is
$$
q=(N_p-N_e)e;\qquad
\mathrm H^+:q=+e,\quad \mathrm H^{\bullet}:q=0,\quad \mathrm H^-:q=-e.
$$
The <electron pair> and <electric charge> questions require different information. An odd $N_e$ ensures that not all <electrons> can be paired; an even $N_e$ alone does not ensure pairing, as the <molecular orbital> description of <molecular oxygen> illustrates.

\Image[radical-charge-and-orbital-occupancy.png]
{id=image-radical-charge-and-orbital-occupancy}
{title=<Electric charge> and unpaired <electrons> are different properties}
{description=Left: actual <ground state> $1s$ occupancies of $\mathrm H^+$, $\mathrm H^{\bullet}$, and $\mathrm H^-$. Right: stipulated two-orbital models with fixed $N_p=2$ illustrate neutral, positive, and negative <free radicals>; those boxes are not assignments for particular <chemical substances>. Arrows label <spin> occupancy, not particle trajectories.}

The distinction matters whenever a <chemical reaction> mechanism uses dots and charge signs together. <Homolysis> gives one bonding <electron> to each fragment, while <heterolysis> assigns both to one fragment. A <radical chain reaction> can pass an unpaired <electron> from one species to another without generating net <electric charge>. Detection then exploits the unpaired <electron>'s <spin> and <magnetic moment>, rather than merely measuring whether the sample carries an <electric charge>.

= Methyl radical
{parent=Radical (chemistry)}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3}^{\bullet}$}

The <methyl radical> is the neutral <radical (chemistry)> $\mathrm{CH_3}^{\bullet}$, with an unpaired <electron>. It carries the <methane chlorination chain> between its two propagation steps.

= Radical ion
{parent=Radical (chemistry)}

A <radical ion> is both a <radical (chemistry)> and an <ion>. Removing one <electron> from neutral <benzene> can produce $\mathrm{C_6H_6}^{\bullet+}$, a <radical cation>; adding one can produce $\mathrm{C_6H_6}^{\bullet-}$, a <radical anion>. These formulae specify net <electric charge> and an unpaired <electron>, without claiming that both reside on one <chemical atom>.<Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry (IUPAC Recommendations 1994): R entries by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry, IUPAC Recommendations 1994: radical ion, benzene examples and dot/charge placement]{ref}

= Radical cation
{parent=Radical ion}

A <radical cation> has positive net <electric charge> and an unpaired <electron>.

= Radical anion
{parent=Radical ion}

A <radical anion> has negative net <electric charge> and an unpaired <electron>.

= Radical chain reaction
{parent=Radical (chemistry)}

A <radical chain reaction> regenerates a <radical (chemistry)> during propagation. Initiation supplies <free radicals>; propagation consumes one and produces another; termination removes chain-carrying <free radicals>. This explains why one initiating event can lead to many product-forming <chemical reactions>. The <chain-growth polymerization> article develops the corresponding growth of <polymers>.

= Methane chlorination chain
{parent=Radical chain reaction}

Light initiates <methane> chlorination by <photodissociation> of $\mathrm{Cl_2}$ through <homolysis>. The elementary bookkeeping is
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{initiation:}\quad &\mathrm{Cl_2}\xrightarrow{h\nu}2\mathrm{Cl}^{\bullet},\\
\text{propagation:}\quad &\mathrm{Cl}^{\bullet}+\mathrm{CH_4}\longrightarrow\mathrm{HCl}+\mathrm{CH_3}^{\bullet},\\
&\mathrm{CH_3}^{\bullet}+\mathrm{Cl_2}\longrightarrow\mathrm{CH_3Cl}+\mathrm{Cl}^{\bullet}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Here $h\nu$ denotes the <photon> <energy>. The first propagation step replaces a <chlorine> <radical (chemistry)> with a <methyl radical>; the second regenerates a <chlorine> <radical (chemistry)>. Adding the two propagation equations cancels both <free radicals> and gives $\mathrm{CH_4+Cl_2\longrightarrow CH_3Cl+HCl}$: <chloromethane> and <hydrogen chloride> are made while the chain carrier is regenerated.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/6.6 Radical Reactions>[Organic Chemistry by John McMurry, §6.6: chlorine photolysis and the two-step methane chlorination cycle]{ref}

\Image[methane-chlorination-radical-bookkeeping.png]
{id=image-methane-chlorination-radical-bookkeeping}
{title=<Methane> chlorination: initiation supplies carriers, propagation replaces them, termination removes them}
{description=The left panel follows the reacting <electron pair> in <homolysis>, then counts chain carriers through two propagation steps. The right panel shows recombination termination. Every <chemical species> in this example is neutral. Electron marks are bookkeeping, not a measured electronic <wavefunction> or a reaction-rate plot.}

For example, recombination $2\mathrm{CH_3}^{\bullet}\longrightarrow\mathrm{C_2H_6}$ of <methyl radicals> makes <ethane> and a <covalent bond> from two unpaired <electrons>, removing two carriers. Recombination of two $\mathrm{Cl}^{\bullet}$ also terminates carriers; in the gas phase, stabilization of the product requires disposal of released <energy>, for example to a third collision partner. The figure omits that partner. Termination competes with propagation, and further chlorination can consume <chloromethane>, so this cycle alone does not predict a pure product or a measured <reaction rate>.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/10.2 Preparing Alkyl Halides from Alkanes: Radical Halogenation>[Organic Chemistry by John McMurry, §10.2, Figure 10.2 and following paragraphs: radical combination terminates the chain; further substitution gives product mixtures]{ref}

= Stable radical
{parent=Radical (chemistry)}

An unpaired <electron> does not set a universal <reaction rate>. A <stable radical> can retain radical character during isolation and storage under specified conditions; a persistent <radical (chemistry)> survives long enough under specified conditions to be observed. These descriptions concern lifetime and handling, rather than immunity to every possible <chemical reaction>. <DPPH> is a practical counterexample to the claim that all <free radicals> react immediately.

Two mechanisms help explain persistence. Spreading electronic character over several <chemical atoms>, represented by <chemical resonance>, can lower the <energy> relative to a localized description. Bulky surrounding groups can impede approach to a reactive site and slow recombination. Lower <energy> and slower <chemical kinetics> are different advantages: neither alone guarantees a long lifetime. A primary computational study explicitly separates these effects, using electronic and geometric descriptors; it does not directly measure every predicted lifetime.<A quantitative metric for organic radical stability and persistence using thermodynamic and kinetic features by Shree Sowndarya S. V. and others>[A quantitative metric for organic radical stability and persistence using thermodynamic and kinetic features, Shree Sowndarya S. V., Peter C. St. John and Robert S. Paton, 2021: Abstract and Introduction, thermodynamic stabilization versus kinetic persistence]{ref}

= 2,2-Diphenyl-1-picrylhydrazyl
{parent=Stable radical}
{wiki}

= DPPH
{c}
{synonym}

<DPPH> is a neutral organic <radical (chemistry)> used as a <spin> reference in electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR), also called electron spin resonance (ESR). Its unpaired <electron> remains bound within the <chemical species>: “free radical” does not mean a freely moving <electron>. The <DPPH electron spin resonance experiment> makes its <magnetic moment> observable.

= DPPH electron spin resonance experiment
{c}
{parent=2,2-Diphenyl-1-picrylhydrazyl}

The University of Iowa's <Electron Spin Resonance, University of Iowa laboratory protocol> (revised July 2007, Experiment and Resonance Data Analysis, printed pp. 2–6) specifies <DPPH> on cotton in a tube inside an RF coil, between <Helmholtz coils>. A bridge monitors RF absorption; an <oscilloscope> records this output and the voltage across a $1\,\Omega$ current-monitor resistor. Sweeping the coil current passes through the resonant <magnetic field>. The protocol describes two dips when the sweep revisits resonance; these are repeated crossings, not two distinct spectral lines.

Its calibration replaces the sample with a <Hall effect> probe, maps the <magnetic field> in $2\,\mathrm{mm}$ steps, and accounts for sample dimensions. At least four RF <frequencies> test the resonance relation. Sweep speed is adjusted to obtain comparable passages. Only a local peak-plus-background fit is prescribed, because the full shape is not Gaussian. The protocol also flags unexplained trailing spikes and possible electronics contributions. These controls constrain field and sweep errors; an empty-tube/cotton blank would be an additional background control, not a reported measurement in this protocol.<Electron Spin Resonance, University of Iowa laboratory protocol>[Electron Spin Resonance, revised July 2007: printed pp. 4–7, sweep behavior, field mapping, local fitting and anomalous spikes]{ref}

For an isolated effective <spin one-half> with dimensionless positive factor $g$, the basic resonance model is
$$
\Delta E=g\mu_{\rm B}|B|=h\nu,\qquad
g=\frac{h\nu}{\mu_{\rm B}|B_{\rm res}|}.
$$
Here $\mu_{\rm B}$ is the <Bohr magneton>, $h$ is the <Planck constant>, $\nu$ is the applied <frequency>, and $B_{\rm res}$ is the calibrated resonant <magnetic field>. A change in <frequency> should shift the resonance field proportionally in this model. The factor $g$ describes the electronic <magnetic moment> coupling, not the species' net <electric charge>.

A resonance supports the presence of detectable <paramagnetism>. It does not, by itself, identify an unknown <radical (chemistry)>: other species exhibiting <paramagnetism>, impurities, overlapping responses, and interactions with <atomic nuclei> can complicate interpretation. Conversely, absence of a detectable resonance need not mean absence of <free radicals>; a low <concentration>, a short lifetime, or an unsuitable measurement range can conceal them. The occupancy and chain diagrams are original bookkeeping illustrations; neither is a measured or simulated <DPPH> spectrum.

\Include[molecule]{parent=Chemistry}

= Quantum chemistry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Electron density
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{title2=$n(\mathbf r)$}

The electron density describes the expected number of <electrons> per unit <volume> at position $\mathbf r$. For a system with a fixed number $N$ of <electrons>, $\int n(\mathbf r)\,d^3\mathbf r=N$. A <Lewis structure> allocates electron counts without specifying this spatial density.

= Electron configuration
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{wiki}

An <electron configuration> records the occupation of <electron shells> and <electron subshells> by <electrons>. For neutral <sodium> in its <ground state>, $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^6\,3s^1$ gives $2+2+6+1=11$ <electrons>; adding the occupations within each <electron shell> gives $(2,8,1)$. A superscript counts <electrons>, rather than multiplying the preceding labels. Removing the outer <electron> gives the <sodium> <ion> $\mathrm{Na}^{+}$ with $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^6$, or $(2,8)$. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-4-electronic-structure-of-atoms-electron-configurations[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, electron configurations of atoms and ions].

= Electron shell
{parent=Electron configuration}
{title2=$n$}
{wiki}

An <electron shell> collects the allowed electronic <quantum states> sharing a <principal quantum number> $n$. Its occupancy is the number of <electrons> assigned to those states in an <electron configuration>. An <electron shell> is not a material surface or a prescribed orbit.

= Electron subshell
{parent=Electron shell}

An <electron subshell> groups the allowed electronic <quantum states> within an <electron shell> by their <orbital angular momentum> quantum number $\ell$. Labels $s$ and $p$ mean $\ell=0$ and $\ell=1$, respectively; their capacities are two and six <electrons>. The leading number in $2p$ identifies the <electron shell>, while $p$ identifies the <electron subshell>.

= Atomic orbital
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\phi_{n\ell m}(\mathbf r)$}

An atomic orbital is a one-<electron> spatial <wavefunction> used to describe an <electron>'s state in a <chemical atom>. For a one-electron <hydrogen atom>, orbitals can be exact <energy eigenstates>; descriptions of many-electron atoms instead use an orbital approximation. A normalized atomic orbital has $\int|\phi|^2\,d^3\mathbf r=1$, and the <Born rule> gives the position <probability density> $|\phi|^2$. It is a function extending through space, not a prescribed classical orbit. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-3-development-of-quantum-theory[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 6.3: atomic orbitals and the wavefunction description]{ref}

The <linear combination of atomic orbitals> combines these single-centre functions to approximate a <molecular orbital>. The <orbital overlap> measures their <inner product>, and their relative <quantum phase> controls the <interference> shown in <molecular orbital theory>.

= Molecular orbital theory
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{wiki}

<Molecular orbital theory> describes the electronic arrangement of a <molecule> using <molecular orbitals>: one-<electron> spatial <wavefunctions> extending over the combined <atomic nuclei>. Filling <bonding molecular orbitals> can stabilize a <chemical bond>; filling <antibonding molecular orbitals> opposes that stabilization. A <molecular orbital> is a building block of a many-<electron> description, rather than the entire many-<electron> <wavefunction>. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/ef9da1132ff6233d732a1d6115099467_MIT5_61F17_lec24.pdf[Robert W. Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 24, pp. 1–4: molecular orbitals and the LCAO approximation].

First make the <interference> visible with an exactly normalized one-dimensional toy. Replace the three-dimensional $1s$ <atomic orbitals> of the <hydrogen atom> by two <Gaussian functions> centred at $x=\pm a$, with <dimensionless> $x$ and $a>0$:
$$
\phi_A(x)=\pi^{-1/4}e^{-(x+a)^2/2},\qquad
\phi_B(x)=\pi^{-1/4}e^{-(x-a)^2/2},\qquad
S=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\phi_A\phi_B\,dx=e^{-a^2}.
$$
The normalized sum and difference are $\psi_\pm=(\phi_A\pm\phi_B)/\sqrt{2(1\pm S)}$, following the same <linear combination of atomic orbitals> algebra. At $a=1$, the <orbital overlap> is $S=e^{-1}\approx0.367879$. At the midpoint, $\phi_A(0)=\phi_B(0)\approx0.455581$, giving
$$
\psi_+(0)\approx0.550879,\qquad |\psi_+(0)|^2\approx0.303468,\qquad
\psi_-(0)=|\psi_-(0)|^2=0.
$$
Both <probability densities> have total <integral> one: <interference> redistributes <probability>, rather than creating an extra <electron>. These are toy <wavefunctions>, not actual <hydrogen atom> orbitals or predictions of <molecular hydrogen> <energies>.

\Image[molecular-orbital-interference.png]
{title=Normalized one-dimensional <Gaussian function> toy: signed <probability amplitudes> add or cancel before their squares give <probability densities>}
{description=Centres at $x=-1$ and $x=1$ give <orbital overlap> $S=e^{-1}$. The difference has a zero at the midpoint; its negative lobe does not carry positive <electric charge>. All density curves shown are normalized on the line, rather than in three-dimensional space.}

Now fill the two $1s$-derived <molecular orbitals> in the minimal model. The lower <bonding molecular orbital> is labelled $\sigma_g$; the upper <antibonding molecular orbital> is $\sigma_u^*$. The <Pauli exclusion principle> permits at most two <electrons> in each spatial <molecular orbital>, with opposite <spin> projections along a chosen axis. For the lowest <electron configuration> in this model, fill $\sigma_g$ first. The subscripts $g$ and $u$ denote <even> and <odd> behaviour under inversion through the midpoint; the star marks an <antibonding molecular orbital>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 8.4: occupancy and bond order] and https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/e9f9413400be87f6a0a512e757d52e3c_MIT5_61F17_lec25.pdf[Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 25, pp. 1–3: bonding, antibonding, and symmetry labels].

With $N_b$ <electrons> in <bonding molecular orbitals> and $N_a$ in <antibonding molecular orbitals>, the model's <bond order> is $b=(N_b-N_a)/2$. The three elementary counts are:
* <Molecular hydrogen>, $\mathrm H_2$: two <electrons> give $(\sigma_g)^2$ and $b=(2-0)/2=1$.
* The <hydrogen> molecular <ion> $\mathrm H_2^+$: one <electron> gives $(\sigma_g)^1$ and $b=(1-0)/2=1/2$.
* Two neutral <helium> <chemical atoms>, $\mathrm{He}_2$: four <electrons> give $(\sigma_g)^2(\sigma_u^*)^2$ and $b=(2-2)/2=0$ in this minimal $1s$ model.

\Image[molecular-orbital-occupancy.png]
{title=Schematic <molecular orbital> occupancy for $\mathrm H_2$, $\mathrm H_2^+$, and $\mathrm{He}_2$: <bond orders> one, one half, and zero}
{description=Each horizontal line is one spatial <molecular orbital>; paired arrows represent opposite <spin> projections. Vertical positions show ordering only, with arbitrary gaps rather than measured <energies> or a common scale across species.}

The zero <bond order> says this minimal model gives no net <covalent bond> for $\mathrm{He}_2$. It does not rule out every bound pair of <helium> <chemical atoms>: a very weak <van der Waals force> can bind a neutral <helium> dimer, which has been observed experimentally. See https://www.atom.uni-frankfurt.de/publications/files/ZellerPNAS-2016.pdf[Zeller et al., Imaging the He2 quantum halo state using a free electron laser, PNAS 113, 14651–14655 (2016), p. 14651: van der Waals binding] and https://scholarworks.gvsu.edu/chm_articles/6/[Luo et al., The weakest bond: Experimental observation of helium dimer, Journal of Chemical Physics 98, 3564–3567 (1993)]. The <bond order> count does not include that weak binding mechanism.

The useful surprise is that sharing alone does not determine a <chemical bond>: the relative <quantum phase> and the occupancy matter together. The same two shapes explain why adding an <electron> to a <bonding molecular orbital> increases the model's <bond order>, while adding one to an <antibonding molecular orbital> decreases it. <Molecular orbital theory> also accounts for the unpaired <electrons> and <paramagnetism> of <molecular oxygen>, which its all-paired <Lewis structure> misses. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory[Chemistry 2e, 8.4: the molecular orbital description of oxygen].

= Molecular orbital
{parent=Molecular orbital theory}
{title2=$\psi(\mathbf r)$}
{wiki}

A <molecular orbital> is a one-<electron> spatial <wavefunction> for the combined <atomic nuclei> of a <molecule>. For a normalized <molecular orbital>, the <Born rule> gives the position <probability density> $|\psi(\mathbf r)|^2$, with $\int_{\mathbb R^3}|\psi|^2\,d^3\mathbf r=1$. The <molecular orbital> is the amplitude, rather than its squared <modulus> or a classical trajectory.

In the independent-orbital approximation, a doubly occupied <molecular orbital> contributes $2|\psi|^2$ to the <electron> number density, whose <integral> is two. An empty <molecular orbital> contributes zero, even though its shape is still part of the model. The <molecular hydrogen> example therefore uses one occupied spatial <molecular orbital> for two <electrons>, distinguished by their <spins>. A negative real <probability amplitude> means a relative <quantum phase> of $\pi$, not positive <electric charge>; multiplying the whole <wavefunction> by $-1$ changes only its <global phase>.

= Bonding orbital
{parent=Molecular orbital}
{title2=$\sigma_g$}
{wiki}

= Bonding molecular orbital
{synonym}

Occupying a <bonding molecular orbital> favours a <chemical bond>. For the two equivalent $1s$ <atomic orbitals> of the <hydrogen atom>, the sum $\sigma_g=\sigma_+$ has constructive <interference> between the <protons>. The resulting <electron> density in that region helps stabilize the combined system. Its lower <energy> in this model follows from the <quantum Hamiltonian>, not from the plus sign alone; see https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/e9f9413400be87f6a0a512e757d52e3c_MIT5_61F17_lec25.pdf[Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 25, pp. 1–2].

For example, $(\sigma_g)^2$ in <molecular hydrogen> supplies $N_b=2$, hence <bond order> one when $N_a=0$. The sum in the <molecular orbital theory> toy has midpoint <probability density> $0.303468$, whereas the difference has zero there. The normalization factors must be included in this comparison.

= Antibonding orbital
{parent=Molecular orbital}
{title2=$\sigma_u^*$}
{wiki}

= Antibonding molecular orbital
{synonym}

Occupying an <antibonding molecular orbital> opposes a <chemical bond>. For equivalent $1s$ <atomic orbitals> of the <hydrogen atom>, the difference $\sigma_u^*=\sigma_-$ vanishes on the plane halfway between the <protons>: every point there is equally far from both centres, so the amplitudes cancel. This nodal plane removes the contribution of this <molecular orbital> to <electron> density on that plane; it does not make an entire finite region empty. The $1s$-derived <antibonding molecular orbital> lies above its <bonding molecular orbital> partner in <energy>. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/e9f9413400be87f6a0a512e757d52e3c_MIT5_61F17_lec25.pdf[Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 25, pp. 1–2].

In the $\mathrm{He}_2$ minimal model, $(\sigma_u^*)^2$ adds $N_a=2$ and cancels the <bond order> contribution of $(\sigma_g)^2$. Opposite signs on the two lobes describe <probability amplitudes>, while both lobes have nonnegative <probability density> after squaring. The star is an orbital label, not an extra <electron> or an <electric charge>.

= Linear combination of atomic orbitals
{parent=Molecular orbital theory}
{wiki}

= LCAO
{c}
{synonym}

The <LCAO> approximation builds a <molecular orbital> as a <linear combination> of single-centre <atomic orbitals>. For two equivalent normalized real $1s$ functions $\phi_A$ and $\phi_B$, choose their signs positive and define their <orbital overlap> $S$. At nonzero separation, $0<S<1$, and the normalized combinations are
$$
\sigma_\pm(\mathbf r)=\frac{\phi_A(\mathbf r)\pm\phi_B(\mathbf r)}{\sqrt{2(1\pm S)}},\qquad
S=\int_{\mathbb R^3}\phi_A(\mathbf r)\phi_B(\mathbf r)\,d^3\mathbf r.
$$
Here $\sigma_+=\sigma_g$ and $\sigma_-=\sigma_u^*$. Equal coefficients use the equivalence of the two centres; a general <LCAO> need not have equal coefficients. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/ef9da1132ff6233d732a1d6115099467_MIT5_61F17_lec24.pdf[Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 24, pp. 3–6: LCAO coefficients and overlap integrals].

For the <Gaussian function> toy's $a=1$, the two denominators are $\sqrt{2(1+S)}\approx1.654013$ and $\sqrt{2(1-S)}\approx1.124385$. To check <wavefunction normalization>, expand the square: $\int(\phi_A\pm\phi_B)^2=1+1\pm2S$. Dividing by $2(1\pm S)$ gives one. The local <probability density> is
$$
|\sigma_\pm|^2=\frac{\phi_A^2+\phi_B^2\pm2\phi_A\phi_B}{2(1\pm S)}.
$$
The cross term is the <interference>; adding the separate densities first would lose it. These formulas normalize the chosen <linear combination> exactly, while the restricted choice of <atomic orbitals> remains an approximation to an actual <molecular orbital>.

= Orbital overlap
{parent=Linear combination of atomic orbitals}
{title2=$S$}

The <orbital overlap> of normalized real <atomic orbitals> is their <inner product> $S=\int\phi_A\phi_B$. It is a <dimensionless> measure of their common amplitude, not an <electron> count. For the <Gaussian function> toy, increasing $a$ from $1$ to $2$ reduces $S$ from $e^{-1}\approx0.367879$ to $e^{-4}\approx0.018316$. As $S$ tends to zero, both <LCAO> normalization denominators tend to $\sqrt2$.

= Bond order
{parent=Molecular orbital theory}
{title2=$b=(N_b-N_a)/2$}
{wiki}

In the elementary <molecular orbital theory> count, <bond order> is half the excess of <electrons> in <bonding molecular orbitals> over those in <antibonding molecular orbitals>. Thus adding one <electron> to a <bonding molecular orbital> raises $b$ by $1/2$, while adding one to an <antibonding molecular orbital> lowers it by $1/2$. The configurations drawn above give $1$, $1/2$, and $0$. This count summarizes net <covalent bond> occupancy; it is neither a measured <energy> nor a universal test for every kind of binding. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory[Chemistry 2e, 8.4: molecular orbital bond order].

\Include[chemical-bond]{parent=Chemistry}

= Molar mass
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$M$}

<Molar mass> turns a weighed sample into a count of specified particles. For a pure <chemical substance> with specified <chemical formula> and <isotopic abundances>, its <molar mass> is
$$
M=\frac{m}{n},\qquad n=\frac{m}{M},
$$
where $m$ is the sample <mass> and $n$ its <amount of substance>. The <SI> unit is $\mathrm{kg\,mol^{-1}}$; the common laboratory unit is $\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$, with $1\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}=10^{-3}\,\mathrm{kg\,mol^{-1}}$. Equal values of <amount of substance> correspond to equal counts of specified entities, but heavier entities give greater <masses>.

Combining this definition with <molecular count and amount of substance> gives
$$
N=\frac{mN_{\mathrm A}}{M},\qquad \bar m_{\rm particle}=\frac{m}{N}=\frac{M}{N_{\mathrm A}},\qquad N_{\mathrm A}=6.02214076\times10^{23}\,\mathrm{mol^{-1}}.
$$
The <Avogadro constant> is exact under the modern definition of the <mole>. The <mass> $M/N_{\mathrm A}$ is the mean <mass> per specified entity; if <isotopes> differ within the sample, individual <molecules> need not all have that mean <mass>.<The International System of Units (SI), ninth edition, SI Brochure>[§2.3.1, The mole, printed p. 131: exact Avogadro constant, specified entities, and M(X) = N_A m(X)]{ref}

Specify what $n$ counts: <water> <molecules>, <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>, and <oxygen> <chemical atoms> give different values of <amount of substance> for the same sample. For a <mixture>, dividing total <mass> by total <amount of substance> of its chosen entities gives a composition-dependent mean. A <polymer> sample can contain many chain <molar masses>; its <number-average molar mass> and <weight-average molar mass> answer different averaging questions. Relating either to <degree of polymerization> also requires a unit-mass convention and end-group accounting.

= Number-average molar mass
{parent=Molar mass}
{title2=$M_n$}

The <number-average molar mass> weights each <molecule> equally. For counts $n_i$ with <molar masses> $M_i$,
$$
M_n=\frac{\sum_i n_iM_i}{\sum_i n_i}.
$$
A mass-proportional <SEC detector response> requires conversion to counts before determining this average.

= Weight-average molar mass
{parent=Molar mass}
{title2=$M_w$}

The <weight-average molar mass> weights each population by its contribution to total <mass>:
$$
M_w=\frac{\sum_i n_iM_i^2}{\sum_i n_iM_i}.
$$
Here $n_i$ counts <molecules> with <molar mass> $M_i$. <SEC light-scattering detection> can infer this average for an eluting slice under its optical and <concentration> assumptions; overlapping species need not have the same individual <molar mass>.

= Relative molecular mass
{parent=Molar mass}
{title2=$M_{\rm r}$}

The <relative molecular mass> is the <dimensionless> ratio of a specified mean <molecule> <mass> to the <unified atomic mass unit>, written $m_{\rm u}$ when treated as a <physical quantity>:
$$
M_{\rm r}=\frac{\bar m_{\rm molecule}}{m_{\rm u}},\qquad M=M_{\rm r}M_{\rm u}.
$$
Thus a <relative molecular mass> of approximately $18.015$ and a <molar mass> of approximately $18.015\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$ describe related quantities with different units. The conversion uses the <molar mass constant>, rather than identifying a <dimensionless> number with a <mass> per <mole>.

= Molar mass constant
{parent=Molar mass}
{title2=$M_{\rm u}=N_{\mathrm A}m_{\rm u}$}

The <molar mass constant> converts <relative atomic mass> or <relative molecular mass> into <molar mass>. It is approximately $1\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$. Its <SI> numerical value is determined experimentally: the exact <Avogadro constant> multiplies an experimentally determined <unified atomic mass unit> in kilograms. Treating $M_{\rm u}$ as exactly $1\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$ is therefore an excellent elementary-calculation approximation, rather than a defining equality of the modern <SI>.<The International System of Units (SI), ninth edition, SI Brochure>[§2.3.1, The mole, printed p. 131: carbon-12 molar mass no longer exact; M_u = N_A m_u and M(X) = A_r(X) M_u]{ref}

= Water mass to molecular count
{parent=Molar mass}

Choose approximate <atomic weights> $A_{\rm r}(\mathrm H)=1.008$ and $A_{\rm r}(\mathrm O)=15.999$, and use $M_{\rm u}\approx1\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$. The <chemical formula> $\mathrm{H_2O}$ then gives <water> <molar mass>
$$
M(\mathrm{H_2O})\approx\bigl(2(1.008)+15.999\bigr)\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}=18.015\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
These are deliberately chosen rounded inputs, not exact <atomic weights> or a universal value for every <water> sample. Different <isotopic abundances>, notably those in <heavy water>, change the mean <molar mass>; <chemical formula> alone does not specify them. Adding <atomic weights> also neglects the tiny <mass> change associated with <chemical bond> <energies>, immaterial at this rounding precision.

For an illustrative pure <water> sample of <mass> $4.50375\,\mathrm g$, this convention gives
$$
n\approx\frac{4.50375\,\mathrm g}{18.015\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}}=0.250000\,\mathrm{mol},\qquad N\approx1.50553519\times10^{23}.
$$
The count refers to <water> <molecules>. Their <hydrogen> <chemical atom> count is approximately $2N=3.01107038\times10^{23}$, and their <oxygen> <chemical atom> count is approximately $N$. The mean <mass> per <water> <molecule> is $M/N_{\mathrm A}\approx2.99146\times10^{-26}\,\mathrm{kg}$. Extra displayed digits expose the arithmetic; they do not imply that the sample composition or <mass> was measured that precisely.

\Image[molar-mass-water-particle-map.png]
{id=molar-mass-water-particle-map}
{title=Mapping <water> <mass> through <amount of substance> to <molecule> and <chemical atom> counts using chosen approximate <atomic weights>}

The same conversion makes <stoichiometry> a <mass> calculation. For the <chemical equation> $2\mathrm{H_2}+\mathrm{O_2}\to2\mathrm{H_2O}$, complete conversion of $0.250000\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular hydrogen> and $0.125000\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular oxygen> gives $0.250000\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <water>. With the chosen approximate <atomic weights>, the reactant <masses> are $0.50400\,\mathrm g$ and $3.99975\,\mathrm g$, summing to $4.50375\,\mathrm g$. The $2:1:2$ ratio belongs to <molecule> counts and <amount of substance>, not to <masses>; this bookkeeping neglects the much smaller reaction <energy> contribution to <mass>.

= Two-population molar mass example
{parent=Molar mass}

Suppose a synthetic <polymer> population contains three chains of <molar mass> $10000\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$ for every one chain of <molar mass> $40000\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$. Multiplying both chain counts by any common factor leaves the averages unchanged. Substitution into the existing <number-average molar mass> and <weight-average molar mass> definitions gives
$$
M_n=\frac{3(10000)+40000}{4}\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}=17500\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}},\qquad M_w=\frac{3(10000)^2+(40000)^2}{3(10000)+40000}\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}\approx27143\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
The larger chains are only one quarter of the chain count but supply $40000/70000=4/7$ of the <mass>. Thus a <weighted mean> that emphasizes <mass> gives a larger result than one that weights each <molecule> equally. Total <mass> divided by total chain <amount of substance> gives $M_n$, while $M_w/N_{\mathrm A}$ is not the mean <mass> of a randomly chosen chain. Inferring chain counts from <size-exclusion chromatography> still requires the existing <SEC detector response> and <SEC molar mass calibration> assumptions.

= Vapor-density molar mass measurement
{parent=Molar mass}

An <ideal gas law> measurement obtains <amount of substance> without knowing the <chemical formula> in advance:
$$
n=\frac{pV}{RT},\qquad M_{\rm inferred}=\frac{mRT}{pV}.
$$
Here $p$ is vapor <pressure>, $V$ is its occupied <volume (thermodynamics)>, $T$ is absolute <temperature>, $R$ is the <molar gas constant>, and $m$ is the <mass> of vapor occupying that <volume (thermodynamics)> at those conditions.

Santa Monica College's teaching experiment uses a $125\,\mathrm{mL}$ <Erlenmeyer flask> with a pinhole foil cap, a $600\,\mathrm{mL}$ water bath on a hot plate, a <thermometer>, and a barometer. Excess volatile liquid displaces air. A dissolved dye loses its colour and deposits when the liquid evaporates; three more minutes of heating follow. Cooling condenses retained vapor. Subtracting the dry assembly <mass> from the cooled assembly <mass> supplies $m$. The flask <volume (thermodynamics)> comes from brim-full <water> <mass> and <mass density> at its measured <temperature>. Controls include a dry exterior, excluding bath <water>, and three trials.<11B: The Dumas Method (Experiment) by Santa Monica College>[Santa Monica College, 11B, Experimental Set-up steps 1–6; Trials 1–3 steps 1–4: apparatus, dye endpoint, heating, drying and repetition]{ref}

The inference assumes vapor <pressure> equals atmospheric <pressure>, vapor and bath reach <thermal equilibrium>, the retained vapor <mass> is recovered on cooling, and the vapor obeys the <ideal gas law>. Residual air, <thermal expansion> of the flask, and vapor <temperature> below bath <temperature> are explicit error questions in the teaching experiment. Repeating trials assesses repeatability; it does not remove a shared bias.<11B: The Dumas Method (Experiment) by Santa Monica College>[Lab Report, questions 1–4: residual air, glass expansion, nonideal vapor and incomplete thermal equilibration]{ref}

= Molar mass measurement model
{parent=Vapor-density molar mass measurement}

For a synthetic illustration, choose $m=0.3000\,\mathrm g$, $V=125.0\,\mathrm{mL}$, $p=101325\,\mathrm{Pa}$ and $T=373.15\,\mathrm K$. Using <molar gas constant> $R\approx8.314462618\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ gives $n\approx0.00408234\,\mathrm{mol}$ and $M_{\rm inferred}\approx73.4873\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$ under the <ideal gas law>. These are chosen model inputs, not observations of a particular <chemical substance>.

\Image[molar-mass-vapor-measurement-model.png]
{id=molar-mass-vapor-measurement-model}
{title=Vapor <molar mass> inference: apparatus, independent readouts, and the assumptions behind a synthetic <ideal gas law> calculation}

An error's direction follows the model. Extra exterior <water> raises the apparent $m$ and hence $M_{\rm inferred}$. If a fraction $f$ of the hot <gas> particles is residual air, and only the unknown vapor contributes to recovered $m$, an otherwise <ideal gas> calculation gives $M_{\rm inferred}=(1-f)M_{\rm true}$. For a pure nonideal vapor, define $Z=pV/(nRT)$; then $M_{\rm inferred}=M_{\rm true}/Z$, so the direction depends on $Z$. The dye endpoint alone cannot establish gas purity, <thermal equilibrium>, or ideality. These conditional checks explain why the same apparatus cannot provide a universal <molar mass> measurement for arbitrary <mixtures> or substances that decompose on heating.

\Include[polymer]{parent=Chemistry}

= Ion
{parent=Chemistry}
{title2=$q=ze$}

An <ion> is a <chemical atom> or a group of <chemical atoms> with a nonzero net <electric charge>. A positive <ion> is a <cation>; a negative <ion> is an <anion>. The whole entity carries the <electric charge>: a <hydronium> <ion> is a group of four <chemical atoms> with net charge $+e$. This definition agrees with https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/I03158[IUPAC Gold Book: ion]{ref}.

For an ordinary <ion> containing only <atomic nuclei> and <electrons>, let $N_p$ count all its <protons> and $N_e$ all its <electrons>. Each <proton> contributes the <elementary charge> $+e$, each <electron> contributes $-e$, and <neutrons> contribute zero. Therefore
$$
q=(N_p-N_e)e=ze,\qquad z=N_p-N_e.
$$
The signed integer $z$ is the net <electric charge> number. Removing an <electron> increases $z$ by one; adding an <electron> decreases $z$ by one. Changing the <electron> count leaves the <atomic number>, and therefore the identity of the <chemical element>, unchanged.

For example, a neutral <sodium> <chemical atom> has $11$ <protons> and $11$ <electrons>. Its <cation> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ still has $11$ <protons>, but only $10$ <electrons>, so $q=(11-10)e=+e$. A neutral <chlorine> <chemical atom> has $17$ <protons> and $17$ <electrons>; its <anion> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ has $17$ <protons> and $18$ <electrons>, so $q=(17-18)e=-e$. These are particle inventories, not pictures of <atomic orbitals>. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/2.6 Ionic and Molecular Compounds> (§2.6: ions and ionic compounds).

\Image[ion-charge-bookkeeping.png]
{title=<Ion> <electric charge> bookkeeping: neutral <sodium> and <chlorine> <chemical atoms> compared with their <cation> and <anion>}
{description=Every colored dot counts one <proton> or one <electron>. Rows are counting arrays, not spatial models of <atomic nuclei> or <electron shells>. The net <electric charges> are $0,+e,0,-e$.}

One <sodium chloride> <formula unit> accounts for one $\mathrm{Na}^+$ <cation> and one $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ <anion>, whose net <electric charges> sum to zero. Ordinary solid <sodium chloride> is an extended arrangement of <ions>, rather than discrete $\mathrm{NaCl}$ <molecules>. This is a useful distinction: a neutral material can contain charged constituents, and its <chemical formula> tells us their ratio without claiming that each pair is a separate <molecule>.

<Ion> bookkeeping connects <chemistry> to <electric current>: moving <ions> can transport <electric charge>, while stationary <ions> cannot supply a transport <current density>. In <electrochemistry>, both signs of <ions> can contribute to the same <conventional current>. An <ion> opposing a fixed <electric charge> is called a <counterion>.

= Polyatomic ion
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}

A <polyatomic ion> consists of a bound group of two or more <chemical atoms> with nonzero total <electric charge>. <Hydronium>, $\mathrm{H_3O^+}$, contains three <hydrogen> and one <oxygen> <chemical atom>; its net <electric charge> is $+e$, where $e$ is the <elementary charge>. This is a discrete charged group, while an <ionic crystal> can contain an extended arrangement of <ions>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/2.6 Ionic and Molecular Compounds>[polyatomic ions, introductory paragraph and Table 2.5, hydronium]{ref}

= Carbonate
{parent=Polyatomic ion}
{title2=$\mathrm{CO}_3^{2-}$}

= Cation
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}

A <cation> is an <ion> with positive net <electric charge>, for example $\mathrm{Na}^+$ or <hydronium>.

= Anion
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}

An <anion> is an <ion> with negative net <electric charge>, for example $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ or <hydroxide>.

= Sulfate
{parent=Anion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{SO}_4^{2-}$}

= Chloride
{parent=Anion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Cl^-}$}

<Chloride> is the <anion> $\mathrm{Cl^-}$, with net <electric charge> $-e$. In the <glycine titration> model, it supplies the negative background <electric charge> introduced with <hydrochloric acid>.

= Permanganate
{parent=Anion}
{title2=$\mathrm{MnO}_4^-$}

A <manganese>-containing <anion> with net <electric charge> $-e$ and <manganese> <oxidation state> $+7$; $e$ is the positive <elementary charge>.

= Nitrate
{parent=Anion}
{title2=$\mathrm{NO}_3^-$}
{wiki}

= Nitrate ion
{synonym}

The <nitrate ion>, $\mathrm{NO}_3^-$, is an <anion> with one <nitrogen> connected to three <oxygen> <chemical atoms> and net <electric charge> $-1$ in units of <elementary charge>. Its 24 <valence electrons> admit three equivalent octet <resonance structures>; see <Lewis structure> for their construction and <chemical resonance> for their interpretation. The N–O connections are <covalent bonds> within the <ion>. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/7.2 Covalent Bonding>.

= Hydrogen ion
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm H^+$}

In <acid-base chemistry>, <hydrogen ion> usually refers to positively charged hydrogen, represented by $\mathrm H^+$. In <water>, this notation describes solvated species, commonly represented by <hydronium>, rather than an isolated bare <proton>. The <hydrogen ion> <thermodynamic activity> defines <pH>.

= Hydronium
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+$}

<Hydronium> is the <ion> formed when a <water> <molecule> accepts a <proton>. It provides the usual aqueous shorthand for the <hydrogen ion> used in <pH> calculations.

= Hydroxide
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{OH}^-$}

= Counterion
{parent=Ion}

A mobile <ion> whose charge has the opposite sign to a charged macromolecule, surface or other fixed species. Its local <concentration> can increase close to that fixed charge; <counterion condensation> distinguishes a bound fraction that remains localized in an infinite-dilution limit.

\Include[acid-base-chemistry]{parent=Chemistry}
\Include[chemical-reaction]{parent=Chemistry}
\Include[electrochemistry]{parent=Chemistry}

= Mixture
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A <mixture> contains two or more <chemical substances> combined in proportions that need not be fixed. A <chemical compound> instead has a definite composition: <water> has two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> per <oxygen> <chemical atom>, whereas a <sucrose> and <water> <mixture> can contain different sugar proportions. A homogeneous <mixture> has uniform composition on the scale of representative samples; a heterogeneous <mixture> has distinguishable regions of different composition. A <chemical solution> is homogeneous, whereas sand dispersed in <water> is a <particle suspension>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/1.2 Phases and Classification of Matter>[Chemistry 2e, §1.2, Classification of Matter: mixtures, variable composition, homogeneous and heterogeneous examples]{ref}

The distinction concerns the sample, not merely its ingredient list. A vessel with dissolved <sodium chloride> and excess undissolved <sodium chloride> contains a <chemical solution> and an <ionic crystal> <thermodynamic phase>. The <liquid> portion can be homogeneous while the whole vessel is heterogeneous. Similarly, <molecular oxygen> dissolved in <water> is part of a <chemical solution>; visible <gas> bubbles introduce a separate <gas> <thermodynamic phase>.

= Diatomaceous earth
{parent=Mixture}
{wiki}

= Diatomite
{synonym}

= Solution
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Mixture}
{wiki}

= Chemical solution
{synonym}

A <chemical solution> is a homogeneous <mixture> in which designated <solutes> are distributed in a <solvent>. Representative equal-<volume (thermodynamics)> samples have the same composition after mixing. Individual <molecules> and <ions> remain distinguishable. The <solute>/<solvent> labels describe their roles in this <mixture>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/3.3 Molarity>[Chemistry 2e, §3.3, Solutions: homogeneity and solvent/solute conventions]{ref}

A dissolved <solute> is dispersed as <molecules>, <chemical atoms>, or <ions>, rather than as the larger <solid> particles of a <particle suspension>. For example, <sucrose> retains its <molecular formula> during <dissolution> in <water>; a <chemical reaction> is not required. Dissolved <sodium chloride> supplies <sodium> <cations> and <chloride> <anions>, surrounded by <water> <molecules> through <solvation>. Dissolved does not mean chemically destroyed or converted into <water>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/11.1 The Dissolution Process>[Chemistry 2e, §11.1, opening molecular and ionic examples and defining traits of solutions]{ref}

\Image[dissolution-particle-identity.png]
{id=image-dissolution-particle-identity}
{title=<Dissolution> disperses particles while preserving their <chemical elements>}
{description=Original schematic with invented particle positions, not a microscopy image. Top: four generic neutral <molecules> retain their identity. Bottom: four <sodium> <cations> and four <chloride> <anions> separate from an <ionic crystal>. Pale dots represent <water> <molecules>; sizes, spacing, and counts are explanatory, not measured. <Solvation> surrounds each dissolved species; the diagram does not specify a <hydration shell> geometry.}

This makes a useful connection between microscopic identity and laboratory sampling. If a <chemical solution> is well mixed, the <amount of substance> in a measured portion follows from its <concentration> and <volume (thermodynamics)>. A scoop from a poorly mixed <particle suspension> need not represent the whole vessel. Visual clarity alone cannot establish composition or exclude a fine dispersion; the quantitative claim needs representative sampling.

= Solution mass and volume bookkeeping
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}
{title2=$c=n/V$}

The denominator of <molar concentration> is the final <volume (thermodynamics)> of the <chemical solution>, including the dissolved <solute>. It is not the <volume (thermodynamics)> of <solvent> initially poured in. A <volumetric flask> fixes the final total <volume (thermodynamics)>; weighing the <solute> supplies its <mass>, and a specified <molar mass> converts that <mass> to <amount of substance>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/3.3 Molarity>[Chemistry 2e, §3.3, molarity definition and Example 3.16: mass converted to moles and divided by total solution volume]{ref}

Consider an original illustrative account, not reported measurements. Suppose $2.922\,\mathrm g$ of pure dry <sodium chloride>, with stipulated <molar mass> $58.44\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$, is completely dissolved and quantitatively transferred into a <chemical solution> whose final <volume (thermodynamics)> is $250.0\,\mathrm{mL}$. Assume no losses, no additional <chemical reactions>, and complete <dissociation of an ionic compound>. Then
$$
n_{\mathrm{NaCl}}=\frac{2.922\,\mathrm g}{58.44\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}}=0.05000\,\mathrm{mol},\qquad
c_{\mathrm{NaCl,analytical}}=\frac{0.05000\,\mathrm{mol}}{0.2500\,\mathrm L}=0.2000\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}.
$$
Here <analytical concentration> counts the <sodium chloride> inventory, not intact dissolved $\mathrm{NaCl}$ <molecules>. Under these assumptions, the <amount of substance> of <sodium> <cations> and of <chloride> <anions> is separately $0.05000\,\mathrm{mol}$; each has <molar concentration> $0.2000\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$. Their combined ionic <amount of substance> is $0.1000\,\mathrm{mol}$, but this does not double either the salt inventory or the <mass>. Each <sodium> <cation> still contains a <sodium> <atomic nucleus>, and each <chloride> <anion> a <chlorine> <atomic nucleus>. Counting <chemical elements> survives <dissociation of an ionic compound>.

The <mass> ledger is different from the <volume (thermodynamics)> ledger. If the final <chemical solution> had an illustrative net <mass> of $252.922\,\mathrm g$, the <water> contribution would be $252.922-2.922=250.000\,\mathrm g$, assuming nothing entered or escaped except those two components. The ratio $2.922/252.922\approx0.011553$ expresses the solute share of total <mass>; the separate <mass density> would be $252.922/250.0=1.011688\,\mathrm{g\,mL^{-1}}$ before rounding. These stipulated quantities are mutually consistent bookkeeping inputs, not a measured <mass density> for saltwater. Neither establishes that $250.0\,\mathrm{mL}$ of <water> was added.

More generally, with full transfer and conserved <solute>, $n=m/M$ and $c=m/(MV)$. No additive <volume (thermodynamics)> or ideal <chemical solution> assumption is needed when $V$ is the measured final <volume (thermodynamics)>. If only a fraction $f$ of the weighed <solute> reaches the flask, the actual result is $c=fm/(MV)$. This short calculation explains why an undissolved residue or an incomplete rinse creates a systematic deficit even when the final calibration mark is read correctly.

= Solution preparation and volumetric calibration
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}

A calibration mark can be tested against an independent <mass> measurement. The Buffalo State-hosted Laboratory Experiment 1. Calibration of Volumetric Glassware uses a $25\,\mathrm{mL}$ <pipette>, filler, <conical flask>, weighing bottle, balance, and thermometer. It specifies clean, freely draining glassware, bubble-free filling, an eye-level <liquid meniscus> reading, gravity drainage, and retaining the residual liquid allowed for by the <pipette>'s calibration. Degassed <water> is delivered to a tared bottle; ten weighings and initial/final <temperature> readings give repeated delivered <volumes> using the <water> <mass density>. Their <mean> and <standard deviation> assess delivery and repeatability. The balance and thermometer must themselves be checked before claiming accuracy. The same handout describes weighing an empty <volumetric flask> and then weighing it filled to its mark.<Calibration of Volumetric Glassware (Buffalo State laboratory handout)>[Laboratory Experiment 1. Calibration of Volumetric Glassware, pp. 1–3: flask principle; Calibrating 25 mL pipette; Calibration Procedure, steps 1–8]{ref}

The independent inference is $V=m_{\mathrm{water}}/\rho_{\mathrm{water}}(T)$. Agreement between repeats constrains scatter; agreement with the nominal delivery additionally needs trustworthy <mass density>, <temperature>, and balance readings. This experiment tests delivered or contained <volume (thermodynamics)> under its conditions. It does not prove that a subsequent <solute> transfer is complete or a prepared <chemical solution> is homogeneous.

The University of Wisconsin–Madison teaching module supplies the complementary preparation demonstration. Dissolved <solute> and vessel rinsings reach the <volumetric flask>; the contents reach room <temperature> before a dropper adjusts the eye-level <liquid meniscus> to the calibration mark. The module excludes a stirring bar during filling because its displaced <volume (thermodynamics)> is absent from the calibration, and requires mixing the neck liquid with the bulk <chemical solution>. Its self-check identifies incomplete <dissolution> and transfer as a cause of lower-than-intended <molar concentration>.<Volumetric Flask: Dissolving a Solute (Wisconsin chemistry teaching module)>[Volumetric Flask: Dissolving a Solute, transfer paragraph and incomplete-dissolution self-check]{ref}<Volumetric Flask: Filling a Volumetric Flask (Wisconsin chemistry teaching module)>[Volumetric Flask: Filling a Volumetric Flask, temperature, displaced volume, and eye-level mark reading]{ref}<Volumetric Flask: Mixing the Solution in a Volumetric Flask (Wisconsin chemistry teaching module)>[Volumetric Flask: Mixing the Solution in a Volumetric Flask, neck/bulk concentration difference]{ref}

The observable endpoints are a liquid boundary at a mark and a delivered <mass>, not a direct count of <solute> particles. An overfilled flask has excessive final <volume (thermodynamics)>; removing some well-mixed <chemical solution> also removes <solute>, so it cannot recover the intended inventory merely by restoring the mark. <Measurement uncertainty>, contamination, incomplete transfer, and sampling before full mixing limit the inferred <molar concentration>. The apparatus demonstrates why the words “dilute to” refer to final <chemical solution> <volume (thermodynamics)>.

= Meniscus
{disambiguate=liquid surface}
{parent=Solution preparation and volumetric calibration}

= Liquid meniscus
{synonym}

A <liquid meniscus> is the curved boundary where a <liquid> meets a vessel wall. In the transparent <water> examples above, the bottom of this curve is aligned with the calibration mark at eye level. Reading from above or below changes the apparent alignment without changing the contained <volume (thermodynamics)>.

= Ionic strength
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}
{wiki}
{title2=$I_c=\frac12\sum_i c_i z_i^2$}

The concentration-based <ionic strength> is $I_c=\frac12\sum_i c_i z_i^2$, where $c_i$ is each dissolved <ion>'s <concentration> and $z_i$ its signed <electric charge> number. It has the same units as $c_i$; some formulas instead use the <dimensionless> ratio $I_c/c^\circ$. All dissolved <ions> contribute, including those that balance the <electric charge> of a <buffer solution>'s <conjugate base>. <Ionic strength> helps describe the interactions represented by <activity coefficients>. See https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/167121/1/BM_5_2020_Unterricht_Hippler_Metcalfe.pdf[Hippler and Metcalfe, activity coefficients and dimensionless ionic strength].

= Solute
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}

A <solute> is a component of a <chemical solution> distinguished from its <solvent>, typically present in a smaller proportion. There can be several <solutes>. A <concentration> must name both what is counted and the denominator: <molar concentration> uses <amount of substance> per final <chemical solution> <volume (thermodynamics)>; a solute-to-solvent <mass> ratio has another denominator.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/3.3 Molarity>[Chemistry 2e, §3.3, Solutions: solute convention and molarity denominator]{ref}

“Added <sodium chloride>” and “dissolved <sodium> <cations>” describe different levels of the same inventory. <Dissociation of an ionic compound> changes the grouping of the particles; it preserves their <chemical elements>. A dissolved <ion> remains an <ion>, and dissolved <sucrose> remains molecular unless a separate <chemical reaction> occurs. Thus the name of the weighed <solute> need not be the name of every dissolved <chemical species>.

= Solvent
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}
{wiki}

A <solvent> is the component designated as the medium of a <chemical solution>, commonly the component present in greatest proportion. <Water> is a common <solvent>, not a necessary one. A <mixture> of mostly <ethanol> with a little <water> can designate <ethanol> as <solvent> and <water> as <solute>. <Chemical solutions> can also be <gas> or <solid> <mixtures>; <molecular nitrogen> is the principal medium of ordinary well-mixed air. When proportions are comparable, state the convention explicitly.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/11.1 The Dissolution Process>[Chemistry 2e, §11.1, paragraph beginning Water is used so often as a solvent; Table 11.1; solvent convention]{ref}

The <solvent> is part of the final sample. Choosing it influences <solubility> and <solvation>; measuring its added <volume (thermodynamics)> alone does not determine the final <chemical solution> <volume (thermodynamics)>. <Solvent> and <solute> <masses> add in a closed account, whereas the final <volume (thermodynamics)> should be measured rather than inferred by adding pre-mixing <volumes>.

= Hydrodynamic size
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}

<Hydrodynamic size> describes the effective dimensions of a dissolved <molecule> moving with its surrounding <solvent>. It depends on <molecular geometry>, internal shape, and <solvent> conditions, rather than just <molar mass>. <SEC pore access> probes size-dependent accessibility of the packing.

= Hydrodynamic radius
{parent=Hydrodynamic size}
{title2=$R_h$}

= Stokes radius
{c}
{synonym}

The <hydrodynamic radius> is the radius of a hypothetical no-slip <sphere> with the same translational <diffusion coefficient> as the dissolved species. In the dilute continuum regime the <Stokes–Einstein relation> gives
$$
R_h=\frac{k_BT}{6\pi\eta D},
$$
where $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant>, $T$ the absolute <temperature>, $\eta$ the <dynamic viscosity> of the <solvent>, and $D$ the translational <diffusion coefficient>. This effective radius need not equal the <radius of gyration> or a geometric molecular radius. It is a useful descriptor for <SEC molar mass calibration>, rather than a complete specification of how every shape enters every pore.

= Dilution
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}

<Dilution> lowers a <solute>'s <concentration> by adding <solvent>. For conserved <amount of substance>, <molar concentration> satisfies
$$
n=c_1V_1=c_2V_2,\qquad c_2=c_1\frac{V_1}{V_2}.
$$
Both $V_1$ and $V_2$ are <chemical solution> <volumes>; $V_2$ is the final total, not the added <solvent>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/3.3 Molarity>[Chemistry 2e, §3.3, Dilution of Solutions: conserved moles and dilution equation]{ref}

The proof is simply the definition $n=cV$ applied before and after. Its useful assumptions are full transfer of the selected portion, no loss or gain of the counted <solute>, and complete mixing. An ideal <chemical solution> approximation is not required for this inventory equation. If <chemical equilibrium> redistributes dissolved <chemical species>, use a conserved <analytical concentration> or <chemical element> inventory rather than assuming each individual species is conserved. This conservation statement does not by itself preserve the <pH>: a <chemical equilibrium> may change as the <chemical solution> is diluted.

= Serial dilution
{parent=Dilution}
{title2=$c_k=c_0\prod_{j=1}^k(v_j/V_j)$}

<Serial dilution> repeatedly transfers a measured portion of one well-mixed <chemical solution> and dilutes that portion to a new final <volume (thermodynamics)>. With transferred <volume (thermodynamics)> $v_j$ and final <volume (thermodynamics)> $V_j$ at step $j$, conserved <solute> gives
$$
n_j=c_{j-1}v_j,\qquad c_j=\frac{n_j}{V_j}=c_{j-1}\frac{v_j}{V_j},\qquad
c_k=c_0\prod_{j=1}^k\frac{v_j}{V_j}.
$$
This <product> follows by repeated substitution. Each step conserves the <solute> in the portion transferred into its receiving flask, not the entire inventory in the preceding flask. Uniform mixing makes the selected fraction representative.

Continue the original <solution mass and volume bookkeeping> example. A $10.00\,\mathrm{mL}$ portion of the $0.2000\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ stock contains $0.002000\,\mathrm{mol}$ of analytical <sodium chloride>, corresponding to $0.11688\,\mathrm g$ before rounding. After <dilution> to $100.0\,\mathrm{mL}$, its <analytical concentration> is $0.02000\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$. A new $5.000\,\mathrm{mL}$ portion of that mixed <chemical solution> contains $0.0001000\,\mathrm{mol}$; <dilution> to $50.00\,\mathrm{mL}$ gives $0.002000\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$. The two factors $10.00/100.0$ and $5.000/50.00$ multiply to $1/100$.

\Image[volumetric-solution-dilution-accounting.png]
{id=image-volumetric-solution-dilution-accounting}
{title=Final <chemical solution> <volume (thermodynamics)> and transferred <solute> in a <serial dilution>}
{description=Original illustrative account, not experimental data. Calibration marks denote final contained <volumes>; arrows denote measured portions transferred. The first arrow carries $0.002000\,\mathrm{mol}$; the second carries $0.0001000\,\mathrm{mol}$. Each receiving flask conserves the inventory in its incoming portion. Equal dilution factors give successively tenfold lower <analytical concentrations>.}

The remaining stock holds $0.05000-0.002000=0.04800\,\mathrm{mol}$; the remaining first <dilution> holds $0.002000-0.0001000=0.001900\,\mathrm{mol}$. Including the final flask restores the starting inventory:
$$
0.04800+0.001900+0.0001000=0.05000\,\mathrm{mol}.
$$
This is a direct check that making a dilute final sample does not destroy the <solute>. Most of it remains in earlier vessels.

<Serial dilution> makes a small final <concentration> accessible using measurable portions instead of a single extremely small transfer. Its limitation is that systematic errors propagate: if each transferred <volume (thermodynamics)> is $1\%$ below its intended value while final <volumes> are correct, two steps give $0.99^2=0.9801$ times the intended <concentration>, a $1.99\%$ deficit. Repeated readings alone do not remove that bias. The gain in manageable <volume (thermodynamics)> therefore depends on calibrated transfer and representative mixing.

= Nuclear magnetic resonance
{parent=Chemistry}

= NMR spectroscopy
{c}
{synonym}

<NMR spectroscopy> measures resonant responses of <atomic nuclei> to applied <electromagnetic radiation> in a <magnetic field>. In <residual-group monitoring of polyurethane formation>, distinguishable signal <integrals> supply a relative residual-group readout; overlap or very weak signals can limit that inference.

= Analytical chemistry
{parent=Chemistry}

The branch of <chemistry> concerned with identifying substances in a sample and measuring their quantities or <concentrations>. A <bicinchoninic acid assay>, for example, estimates <protein> content through an optical measurement.

= Bicinchoninic acid assay
{parent=Analytical chemistry}

An assay that estimates <protein> <concentration> from a colour-producing <chemical reaction>, measured by <absorbance> and compared with known <protein> reference samples. <Bovine serum albumin> is commonly used as the reference <protein>. The readout estimates <protein> content rather than identifying each <protein> in a <mixture>.

= Chromatography
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

<Chromatography> separates components of a <mixture> through different migration rates in contact with a <chromatographic stationary phase> while a <chromatographic mobile phase> transports them. <Liquid chromatography> describes the physical state of the transporting phase; <size-exclusion chromatography> describes a separation mechanism. Classification by phase and classification by mechanism answer different questions. In ideal <size-exclusion chromatography>, differential access to liquid-filled pores replaces binding as the source of differential migration.

= Chromatographic mobile phase
{parent=Chromatography}

The <chromatographic mobile phase> carries <solutes> through the apparatus. In <liquid chromatography> it is a liquid <solvent> or <chemical solution>.

= Chromatographic stationary phase
{parent=Chromatography}

The <chromatographic stationary phase> remains in place during separation. A porous packing in <size-exclusion chromatography> supplies internal liquid regions with size-dependent accessibility; ideal separation requires no <adsorption> of the <solute> to the packing.

= Liquid chromatography
{parent=Chromatography}

= Size-exclusion chromatography
{parent=Liquid chromatography}
{wiki}

= SEC
{c}
{synonym}

= Gel filtration
{synonym}

= Gel permeation chromatography
{synonym}

<Size-exclusion chromatography> separates dissolved <molecules> by their <hydrodynamic size> in the chosen <solvent>. A larger species that cannot enter the packing's pores travels with the liquid between particles and elutes earlier. Smaller species explore more internal liquid and elute later. The column is not a sieve that permanently traps the large species: exclusion makes them leave first. “<Gel filtration>” is common for aqueous separations; “<gel permeation chromatography>” is common for organic-solvent analysis of <polymers>. <SEC molar mass calibration> translates size-dependent elution into a standard-relative estimate of <molar mass>, rather than a universal absolute measurement.

This makes <SEC> useful for separating <proteins> from small dissolved species, comparing <protein aggregation>, and characterizing dissolved <polymer> chains. The <residual-group monitoring of polyurethane formation> experiment already illustrates <polymer> calibration: its reported <molar masses> are relative to <polystyrene> standards. <Size-exclusion characterization of chain polymers> explains why an elution trace alone cannot count chains or establish their growth mechanism.

= SEC pore access
{c}
{parent=Size-exclusion chromatography}

In ideal <SEC pore access>, geometric exclusion changes the fraction of pore liquid a <solute> can occupy. <Diffusion> permits entry into and exit from accessible pores; the through-flow chiefly transports the sample between packing particles. A <hydrodynamic radius> is a useful size descriptor, but actual pore entry also depends on shape and pore geometry. A flexible <polymer> coil is not a rigid ball with a perfectly sharp entry diameter.

\Image[size-exclusion-chromatography-pore-paths.png]
{id=image-size-exclusion-chromatography-pore-paths}
{title=<SEC pore access>: excluded species leave before species that explore pore liquid}
{description=Original schematic of a porous packing and representative excursions. Colored routes distinguish exclusion, partial access, and extensive access; they are not measured trajectories or a calculation of travel times. The circles represent effective <hydrodynamic sizes>, not actual <molecular geometry>. There is no <adsorption> in this illustration.}

The extra delay is best understood as extra accessible storage, rather than a prescribed longer streamline. Pore visits are reversible and random. An entirely excluded species samples the <SEC void volume>; a sufficiently small nonbinding species samples the <SEC total accessible liquid volume>. Intermediate species lie between these limits. Larger <hydrodynamic size> generally means earlier elution within the column's <SEC fractionation range>.

= SEC void volume
{c}
{parent=Size-exclusion chromatography}
{title2=$V_0$}

The <SEC void volume> $V_0$ is the interparticle liquid volume, operationally measured by a sufficiently large excluded, nonbinding tracer. It excludes pore liquid. Choose a tracer that does not undergo <adsorption> or change the packing; correct the measured elution for tubing and detector delay before identifying it with a column-only $V_0$.

= SEC total accessible liquid volume
{c}
{parent=Size-exclusion chromatography}
{title2=$V_t=V_0+V_i$}

The <SEC total accessible liquid volume> is $V_t=V_0+V_i$: interparticle plus pore liquid. A fully included nonbinding tracer estimates it. Geometric bed volume instead includes solid matrix, $V_c=V_t+V_{\rm matrix}$.<Size Exclusion Chromatography: Principles and Methods by GE Healthcare>[GE Healthcare, Size Exclusion Chromatography: Principles and Methods, revision 18102218 AM, printed pp. 15–16, Figure 1.7: liquid, pore and geometric volumes]{ref}

= SEC partition coefficient
{c}
{parent=Size-exclusion chromatography}
{title2=$K_d=(V_e-V_0)/(V_t-V_0)$}

For ideal <SEC>, define the <SEC partition coefficient> by
$$
K_d=\frac{\overline c_{\rm pore}}{c_{\rm interparticle}},\qquad
V_e=V_0+K_dV_i,\qquad
K_d=\frac{V_e-V_0}{V_t-V_0}.
$$
Here $\overline c_{\rm pore}$ averages <solute> <concentration> over all pore liquid, including inaccessible regions. $V_e$ is column elution <volume (thermodynamics)>. Pure steric exclusion gives $0\le K_d\le1$; the endpoints mean exclusion and full access.<Size Exclusion Chromatography: Principles and Methods by GE Healthcare>[GE Healthcare, Size Exclusion Chromatography: Principles and Methods, printed pp. 15–16: concentration-ratio definition and Kd limits]{ref}

A storage argument explains the relation. Assume a dilute stable <solute>, unchanged packing and <solvent>, constant <volumetric flow rate> $Q$, rapid local <chemical equilibrium> between pore liquid and interparticle liquid, negligible binding, and negligible transport through the pores by bulk flow. At interparticle <concentration> $c$, the column holds an <amount of substance> $n=cV_0+K_dcV_i$. Its exit transports $Qc$ per unit time. The ratio $n/(Qc)$ is the mean residence time in this equilibrium transport model; multiplying by $Q$ gives $V_e=V_0+K_dV_i$. For a narrow symmetric <SEC chromatogram> peak, the peak maximum approximates that mean; a strongly asymmetric peak needs an explicitly stated location convention.

The handbook also uses an operational normalization
$$
K_{av}=\frac{V_e-V_0}{V_c-V_0}
=K_d\frac{V_i}{V_i+V_{\rm matrix}}.
$$
This denominator includes solid matrix, so full access gives $K_{av}<1$ when $V_{\rm matrix}>0$. It differs from the pore-liquid <SEC partition coefficient>. Read each source's volume convention before comparing coefficients.<Size Exclusion Chromatography: Principles and Methods by GE Healthcare>[GE Healthcare, Size Exclusion Chromatography: Principles and Methods, printed p. 16, Figure 1.8: Kav uses Vc, not Vt]{ref}

For an original numerical example, take $V_0=5\,\mathrm{mL}$, $V_i=8\,\mathrm{mL}$, and $V_{\rm matrix}=3\,\mathrm{mL}$. Then $V_t=13\,\mathrm{mL}$ and $V_c=16\,\mathrm{mL}$. Elution at $9\,\mathrm{mL}$ gives $K_d=1/2$ but $K_{av}=4/11$. Even a fully included tracer gives $K_{av}=8/11$, not $1$. These are stipulated volumes, not experimental measurements.

= SEC fractionation range
{c}
{parent=Size-exclusion chromatography}

The <SEC fractionation range> has differential <SEC pore access>. Larger excluded species share $V_e\simeq V_0$; fully included species share $V_e\simeq V_t$. Neither group can be size-ranked by elution. A broader pore distribution can trade separation per size increment for range.<Size Exclusion Chromatography: Principles and Methods by GE Healthcare>[GE Healthcare, Size Exclusion Chromatography: Principles and Methods, printed pp. 17–18, Figures 1.9–1.10: selectivity curves, fractionation and exclusion limits]{ref}

= SEC molar mass calibration
{c}
{parent=Size-exclusion chromatography}

<SEC molar mass calibration> measures standards under the same column, <solvent>, and <temperature> conditions as the unknown. Over a suitable fractionation interval, a common empirical approximation is
$$
\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{M_{\rm app}}{M_{\rm ref}}\right)=a-bV_e,\qquad b>0,
$$
where $M_{\rm ref}$ specifies the <molar mass> unit and $b$ has inverse-<volume (thermodynamics)> units. Interpolation is conditional on the standards' size–<molar mass> relationship. <SEC> measures access associated with <hydrodynamic size>, so a branched <polymer>, an extended chain, and a compact <protein> need not share that relationship.

\Image[size-exclusion-chromatography-theoretical-calibration.png]
{id=image-size-exclusion-chromatography-theoretical-calibration}
{title=Theoretical <SEC chromatogram> peaks and a stipulated size-to-<molar mass> calibration}
{description=Original model, not measured data: $V_0=5\,\mathrm{mL}$, $V_t=13\,\mathrm{mL}$, $K_d=\operatorname{clip}(1-\log_4(R_h/\mathrm{nm}),0,1)$, and standard-family $M=10^4(R_h/\mathrm{nm})^2\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$. Equal-area <Gaussian distribution> peaks have a chosen width $0.28\,\mathrm{mL}$ and equal detector response factors. The lower curve includes model exclusion and full-access plateaus; its secondary mass scale applies only to the stipulated standards.}

In this model the $80{,}000$, $40{,}000$, and $20{,}000\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$ standards have <hydrodynamic radii> $2\sqrt2$, $2$, and $\sqrt2\,\mathrm{nm}$ and elute at $7$, $9$, and $11\,\mathrm{mL}$. An unknown at $8\,\mathrm{mL}$ would be assigned $M_{\rm app}=40{,}000\sqrt2\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$ by this calibration. But a $40{,}000\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$ species that expands from $2$ to $2\sqrt2\,\mathrm{nm}$ elutes at $7\,\mathrm{mL}$ and is assigned $80{,}000\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$ without gaining any <mass>. The numbers demonstrate ambiguity, rather than predict a real unfolding transition.

Changes in <conformational isomerism>, <protein folding>, <protein denaturation>, and <protein aggregation> can therefore shift peaks. Association usually increases size; compaction can reduce size at unchanged <molar mass>. <Adsorption> creates a different ambiguity: delayed elution need not indicate a smaller species. A different <solvent> can change both <polymer> coil dimensions and interactions with the packing. The <SEC protein conformation experiment> tests these alternatives with additional detectors.

= SEC chromatogram
{c}
{parent=Size-exclusion chromatography}

A <SEC chromatogram> plots a detector signal against elapsed time or elution <volume (thermodynamics)>. At constant <volumetric flow rate> $Q$, the volume delivered after injection is $Q(t-t_{\rm injection})$; subtraction of extra-column transport volume is needed for a column-only $V_e$. Real peak widths include injection volume, column dispersion, and tubing/detector spreading. Peak position, width, and area answer different questions.

= SEC detector response
{c}
{parent=SEC chromatogram}

A <SEC detector response> must be specified before interpreting a peak area. <Ultraviolet> <absorbance> depends on a species' response factor and <concentration>, as described by the <Beer-Lambert law>; differential <refractive index> detection depends on how the dissolved species changes <refractive index>. Equal amounts need not produce equal signal areas across unlike species.

Even a calibrated mass-proportional trace is not a chain-count <probability distribution>. In an original two-population example, equal recovered <masses> of chains with <molar masses> $20{,}000$ and $80{,}000\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$ contain four times as many chains in the lighter population. Thus equal signal areas, assuming equal response per unit <mass>, correspond to number fractions $4/5$ and $1/5$: their <number-average molar mass> is $32{,}000\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$, while their <weight-average molar mass> is $50{,}000\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$. Converting a continuous trace additionally requires response correction and the appropriate <change of variables> between elution and <molar mass> bins.

= SEC light-scattering detection
{c}
{parent=Size-exclusion chromatography}

<SEC light-scattering detection> adds information from <scattering of light> to the separation. Static scattering with a <concentration> readout can infer a <weight-average molar mass> for material in an eluting slice without the standard-family retention calibration. Dynamic scattering estimates a <diffusion coefficient> and hence a <hydrodynamic radius>. “Absolute” here means independent of a retention-to-<molar mass> standard curve; it still needs calibrated detectors, optical response parameters, and a valid sample model. Coeluting species produce a mixture-weighted result, rather than individually identified <molecules>.

A concrete manufacturer test used a Bio SEC-3 $300\,\mathrm{\mathring A}$ column, $7.8\times300\,\mathrm{mm}$ with $3\,\mathrm{\mu m}$ particles, an Agilent 1260 Infinity G5611A quaternary pump and G5667A autosampler, a $10\,\mathrm{mm}$ diode-array flow cell, and dual-angle/dynamic scattering detectors. A <buffer solution> at <pH> $7.4$ flowed at $0.75\,\mathrm{mL\,min^{-1}}$; column and scattering detector were at $30\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. <Absorbance> at $280\,\mathrm{nm}$ supplied a concentration-sensitive readout. <Dilution> and loading comparisons tested response: seventeen ovalbumin injections spanning $2$–$960\,\mathrm{\mu g}$ gave a reported average $47{,}198\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$ with $2\%$ relative standard deviation. The note reports poorer separation of thyroglobulin from aggregates and an elevated mass estimate, illustrating the coelution limit. Its $0.2\,\mathrm{\mu m}$ sample filtration also limits interpretation of larger <protein aggregation> products removed before injection.<Determination of Protein Molecular Weight and Size Using the Agilent 1260 Infinity Multi-Detector Bio-SEC Solution with Advanced Light Scattering Detection by Sonja Schneider>[Sonja Schneider, Determination of Protein Molecular Weight and Size Using the Agilent 1260 Infinity Multi-Detector Bio-SEC Solution with Advanced Light Scattering Detection, 5991-3955EN, printed pp. 2–4: apparatus, Table 1, Figure 2 and Table 3]{ref}

= SEC protein conformation experiment
{c}
{parent=SEC light-scattering detection}

An authored manufacturer experiment used an Agilent 1260 Infinity Bio-inert Quaternary LC with Bio SEC-3 $300\,\mathrm{\mathring A}$ columns, $7.8\,\mathrm{mm}$ diameter and $150$ or $300\,\mathrm{mm}$ length. Four pump channels mixed <water>, <sodium chloride>, and two <phosphate> solutions into $20\,\mathrm{mmol\,L^{-1}}$ <phosphate> at <pH> $7.0$, varying salt from $0.2$ to $0.6\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$. At $1.0\,\mathrm{mL\,min^{-1}}$ and $30\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, the readouts were diode-array <absorbance>, differential <refractive index>, and scattering at $15^\circ$ and $90^\circ$. A supplied <protein> standard mixture calibrated retention; bovine serum albumin calibrated scattering constants and inter-detector delay. Increasing salt improved separation of two α-chymotrypsinogen A peaks; two $300\,\mathrm{mm}$ columns also resolved peaks that one column did not. At $0.5\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ salt, the two scattering-derived estimates were about $26{,}100$ and $25{,}800\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$. The author inferred a possible size-changing conformation difference, rather than doubling of <molar mass>. The same poster shows delayed, tailing <lysozyme> peaks from unwanted interactions.<Size Exclusion Chromatography of Biomolecules – Detector Combinations to Obtain Maximum Information with Minimum Effort by Andrew Coffey>[Andrew Coffey, Size Exclusion Chromatography of Biomolecules – Detector Combinations to Obtain Maximum Information with Minimum Effort, HPLC 2013, poster 901: Experimental; Figures 3–7; Table 1]{ref}

The salt comparison and the independent mass readout constrain interpretation, but do not uniquely prove a particular <protein structure>. Salt can change <protein folding>, <protein aggregation>, and packing interactions together. In ideal <SEC> there is no <adsorption>; attraction from the <hydrophobic effect> or forces described by <electrostatics> can cause additional retention, tailing, and loss of recovered sample. Repulsion can instead reduce pore entry. A peak beyond $V_t$ contradicts the simple nonbinding model, rather than extending its size calibration.

Useful proposed controls compare a <solvent> blank, fresh calibration standards bracketing the sample, several injection amounts and volumes, and recovered sample quantity with injected quantity using an appropriately calibrated detector. Keep <temperature> and <volumetric flow rate> fixed while testing <pH> or <ionic strength>. Compare an independent mass readout before declaring an early peak an aggregate. Loss of an insoluble species during filtration, on-column association or dissociation, overloading, and broad unresolved peaks can all hide the original sample population. These are interpretation checks and limitations, not measurements invented for the figures.

= Infrared spectroscopy
{parent=Chemistry}

= IR spectroscopy
{c}
{synonym}

<Infrared spectroscopy> measures a sample's response to <infrared> <electromagnetic radiation>. Absorption associated with stretching or bending motions of <chemical bonds> helps identify groups and their environments. Peak positions are commonly reported as reciprocal <wavelength>, in $\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$; a lower value means lower <frequency>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/12-6-infrared-spectroscopy[McMurry: infrared spectroscopy and its wavelength convention]{ref}

= Stereochemistry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Stereochemical
{synonym}

<Stereochemistry> studies the spatial arrangement of <chemical atoms> in <molecules>. A <Fischer projection> records this information at successive <stereocenters>, whereas a <molecular formula> only counts <chemical atoms>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-2-representing-carbohydrate-stereochemistry-fischer-projections[McMurry: representing carbohydrate stereochemistry].

= Chirality
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Stereochemistry}
{wiki}

= Chiral
{synonym}

<Chirality (chemistry)> means that a <molecule> cannot be superposed on its mirror image. A <carbon> attached to four different groups is a common source of <chirality (chemistry)>; the definition concerns the whole <molecule>, rather than requiring this particular structural feature.

The test is spatial: apply a <translation (geometry)> and a <rotation (mathematics)> to the entire <molecule> until corresponding <chemical atoms> coincide, keeping their identities and <chemical bonds>. A mirror operation is a <reflection (mathematics)>; physically turning a model is a <rotation (mathematics)>. They are different operations. If no such superposition exists, the two forms are <enantiomers>; if one succeeds, the <molecule> is <achiral>. A different-looking flat drawing alone establishes neither conclusion.

For a simple <molecule> with exactly one tetrahedral <stereocenter> and four constitutionally different <substituents>, the mirror forms cannot superpose. Compare entire attached groups, including paths around a ring, rather than just the first <chemical atom>. Several tetrahedral <stereocenters> require a whole-<molecule> check: a <meso compound> can be <achiral>. Conversely, <axial chirality> needs no tetrahedral <stereocenter>.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/5.2 The Reason for Handedness in Molecules: Chirality>[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §5.2: whole-molecule chirality, four different groups and equivalent ring paths]{ref}

\Image[tetrahedral-enantiomer-mirror-geometry.png]
{title=Spatial mirror pair with four distinct substituents}
{description=Ideal <tetrahedral molecular geometry> with distinct <substituents> A, B, C and D. The two models use the same viewing direction and the <reflection (mathematics)> $x\mapsto-x$. Their opposite signed <determinants> exclude superposition by any label-preserving <rotation (mathematics)>. The letters do not denote <R and S configuration> priorities.}

Why care about this distinction? Two <enantiomers> can fit differently into an already <chiral molecule>, just as two opposite hands fit differently into one glove. Thus a <protein>'s spatial arrangement can distinguish a mirror pair even when an <achiral> measurement cannot. <Polarimetry> provides a different kind of test: it measures a sample's net <optical rotation>, which depends on the populations of its <enantiomers>.

= Enantiomer
{parent=Chirality (chemistry)}
{wiki}

<Enantiomers> are a pair of nonsuperposable mirror-image <molecules>. The D and L configurations of <alanine> are an example; <glycine> has no such pair because its <amino acid alpha carbon> carries two identical <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.1, discussion of glycine and Fischer projections after Table 26.1]{ref}

The word names a relation between two <stereoisomers>. Merely finding two different spatial arrangements does not make them <enantiomers>: arrangements that are different but not mirror partners are <diastereomers>.

= Racemate
{parent=Enantiomer}

A <racemate> contains the same <amount of substance> of each member of an <enantiomer> pair. Their equal and opposite contributions cancel in ordinary solution <polarimetry> under identical conditions. Zero net <optical rotation> therefore does not establish <achirality> of the individual <molecules>.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/5.8 Racemic Mixtures and the Resolution of Enantiomers>[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §5.8: equal enantiomer populations and cancelling optical rotations]{ref}

= Chiral molecule
{parent=Chirality (chemistry)}

A <chiral molecule> fails the whole-<molecule> mirror-superposition test defining <chirality (chemistry)>.

= Achirality
{parent=Chirality (chemistry)}

= Achiral
{synonym}

<Achirality> means that a <molecule> is superposable on its mirror image. A <reflection (mathematics)> plane that leaves the complete labelled spatial structure unchanged establishes <achirality>. Absence of such a plane alone is not a complete test: other orientation-reversing spatial symmetries can also make a rigid structure <achiral>.

= Tetrahedral mirror-image test
{parent=Chirality (chemistry)}

Take a central <chemical atom> at the origin and four <substituents> at the vertices of an ideal <regular tetrahedron>:
$$
a=(1,1,1),\quad b=(1,-1,-1),\quad c=(-1,1,-1),\quad d=(-1,-1,1).
$$
These dimensionless <coordinates> describe shape, not measured <bond lengths>. For distinct labels A, B, C and D, define a signed <determinant>
$$
\Delta=\det\begin{pmatrix}|&|&|\\a-d&b-d&c-d\\|&|&|\end{pmatrix}=+16.
$$
It is six times the <oriented volume> of the labelled <tetrahedron>. A <translation (geometry)> cancels from the differences. A <rotation (mathematics)> $Q$ has $\det Q=+1$ and preserves $\Delta$, whereas the mirror <matrix> $S=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1)$ has $\det S=-1$ and changes it to $-16$. Consequently no <rotation (mathematics)> can place every distinct label on its mirror partner. This argument uses three-dimensional <coordinates>, not clockwise order on a page.

Now let C and D both be the same <substituent> H. Swapping these identical groups is allowed. For this model the half-turn
$$
Q=\begin{pmatrix}-1&0&0\\0&0&1\\0&1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad Q^{\mathsf T}Q=I,\qquad\det Q=+1,
$$
about the axis $(0,1,1)$ maps the reflected A and B to their original positions and exchanges the two H positions. The models superpose. With distinct C and D, the same half-turn fails precisely at those two labels; the <determinant> argument rules out every other <rotation (mathematics)> as well. This is the local reason two identical <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> prevent <glycine>'s <amino acid alpha carbon> from being a tetrahedral <stereocenter>.

\Image[tetrahedral-chiral-achiral-rotation-test.png]
{title=The same spatial half-turn succeeds only when the exchanged groups are identical}
{description=Original model, mirror model and rotated mirror model. Top: the distinct C and D remain mismatched, so the A/B/C/D model is <chiral>. Bottom: the identical H groups may exchange, so the A/B/H/H model is <achiral>. The operation is an actual three-dimensional $180^\circ$ <rotation (mathematics)> about $(0,1,1)$, with <determinant> $+1$.}

For a flexible <molecule>, also distinguish <conformational isomerism> from a persistent <enantiomer> pair. A particular frozen shape may be <chiral> while ordinary internal motions interconvert mirror shapes. Turning the entire model is always allowed in a spatial comparison; an internal motion must be considered with its actual accessibility and timescale.

= Meso compound
{parent=Chirality (chemistry)}
{wiki}

A <meso compound> is an <achiral> <molecule> despite having <stereocenters>. <Tartaric acid>, $\mathrm{HOOC{-}CH(OH){-}CH(OH){-}COOH}$, has two tetrahedral <carbon> <stereocenters>. Its $(2R,3R)$ and $(2S,3S)$ forms are <enantiomers>, but $(2R,3S)$ and $(2S,3R)$ name the same meso form after equivalent ends are identified. A symmetric spatial arrangement has a mirror plane exchanging its two halves. Thus there are three <stereoisomers>, not four.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/5.7 Meso Compounds>[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §5.7, Figure 5.12: meso-tartaric acid and its symmetry plane]{ref}

Opposite <R and S configurations> alone do not guarantee a <meso compound>: the rest of the <molecule> must permit mirror superposition. Likewise, a <meso compound> is one <achiral> <chemical compound>, whereas a <racemate> is a mixture of two <chiral molecule> populations. Both can give zero <optical rotation> for different reasons.

= Axial chirality
{parent=Chirality (chemistry)}

An <allene> has a $\mathrm{C=C=C}$ unit whose terminal <substituents> lie in perpendicular planes. In $\mathrm{abC=C=Ccd}$, with $a\ne b$ and $c\ne d$, the two terminal pairs can define <enantiomers> without any tetrahedral <carbon> <stereocenter>. The groups need be different within each end pair, not all four mutually different. This is <axial chirality>.<Basic Terminology of Stereochemistry by G. P. Moss/Axial Chirality>[Basic Terminology of Stereochemistry, entry Axial Chirality: nonplanar pairs about an axis, including allenes]{ref}

= Optical activity
{parent=Chirality (chemistry)}

<Optical activity> is the ability to rotate the direction of <linear polarization> of transmitted <light>. In an <achiral> <solvent>, the two members of an <enantiomer> pair have equal and opposite <optical rotations> at the same <wavelength>, <temperature> and <concentration>. The measured angle concerns a bulk sample; it is not a geometric rotation of the <molecules>.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/5.3 Optical Activity>[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §5.3, Figure 5.6 and specific-rotation discussion]{ref}

= Optical rotation
{parent=Optical activity}
{title2=$\alpha$}

<Optical rotation> is the signed <angle> measured in <polarimetry>. Positive rotation is clockwise for an observer facing the incoming <light>; negative rotation is counterclockwise. State the viewing convention, <wavelength>, <temperature>, <solvent>, <concentration> and path length when comparing measurements. <R and S configuration> specifies spatial arrangement, and does not predict the sign of <optical rotation>.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/5.5 Sequence Rules for Specifying Configuration>[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §5.5, discussion after Figure 5.10: no simple relation between R/S and rotation sign]{ref}

= Specific rotation
{parent=Optical rotation}
{title2=$[\alpha]_{\lambda}^{T}$}

For a dilute <chemical solution> in the usual linear regime,
$$
\alpha=[\alpha]_{\lambda}^{T}\,l\,c,
$$
where $l$ is path length in $\mathrm{dm}$ and $c$ is mass <concentration> in $\mathrm{g\,mL^{-1}}$. These conventions give <specific rotation> units $\mathrm{deg\,dm^{-1}\,mL\,g^{-1}}$. Specify <solvent> as well as <temperature> $T$ and <wavelength> $\lambda$. <Specific rotation> is conditional on these settings, not a universal number independent of the sample environment.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/5.3 Optical Activity>[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §5.3: observed rotation divided by path length and mass concentration]{ref}

For an ideal additive mixture of just two <enantiomers>, write $k$ for the <specific rotation> of the positive member under the chosen conditions and $c_+,c_-$ for their mass <concentrations>. Then $\alpha=kl(c_+-c_-)$, while total mass <concentration> is $c_++c_-$. Equal populations give zero without either member losing its <chirality (chemistry)>. A small signal can also mean low <concentration> or weak <optical rotation> at the selected <wavelength>; other sample components can cancel or alter the signal. Inferring composition from $\alpha$ requires a validated mixture model and a reference measured under matching conditions.

= Polarimetry
{parent=Optical activity}

<Polarimetry> measures <optical rotation> with a <polarimeter>. A concrete modern experiment uses the vertical Vernier Go Direct instrument: a $589\,\mathrm{nm}$ light source, fixed <polarizer>, sample cell, motor-driven analyzer, <photodetector> and optical <rotary encoder>. Its readout is transmitted illumination versus analyzer <angle>. Calibrate with the same <solvent> filled to $10\,\mathrm{cm}$, then rinse with sample and fill to $10.0\,\mathrm{cm}$, reading the bottom of the meniscus to $0.1\,\mathrm{cm}$. The shifted maximum nearest zero gives $\alpha$; a fit with the square of a <cosine> uses the whole trace.<Go Direct Polarimeter User Manual by Vernier>[Go Direct Polarimeter User Manual, Using the Product steps 2–4; Specifications; How the Sensor Works]{ref}

In an ideal optical model, <Malus's law> gives $I(\theta)=I_{\max}\cos^2(\theta-\alpha)$ after solvent zeroing. Maxima repeat every $180^\circ$, so tracking the same peak matters. As an illustrative calculation, not a reported measurement, a peak shift from $0^\circ$ to $+5^\circ$ at $l=1.00\,\mathrm{dm}$ and $c=0.100\,\mathrm{g\,mL^{-1}}$ gives $[\alpha]=+50\,\mathrm{deg\,dm^{-1}\,mL\,g^{-1}}$. This calculation says nothing about <R and S configuration>.

The solvent blank controls the baseline. A reference sample and a repeat blank can check scale and drift; record <temperature>, <concentration> and fill height. Use a transparent homogeneous <chemical solution>, and remove bubbles and particles. The manual distinguishes $\pm0.1^\circ$ fit reproducibility from its $\pm1^\circ$ accuracy specification, and warns that a failed fit can give an unreasonable angle. A precise-looking fit therefore need not be accurate.<Go Direct Polarimeter User Manual by Vernier>[Go Direct Polarimeter User Manual, cosine-squared fit in Using the Product; Specifications; Troubleshooting]{ref} Neither a zero reading nor a matching <specific rotation> uniquely identifies a <molecule>; <polarimetry> must be interpreted with the sample composition and these controls.

= Polarimeter
{parent=Polarimetry}

A <polarimeter> uses <polarizers> to compare the direction of <linear polarization> before and after <light> passes through a sample; <polarimetry> explains the apparatus, readout and controls.

= Stereoisomer
{parent=Stereochemistry}

<Stereoisomers> have the same <chemical bond> connectivity but different spatial arrangements. A mirror pair of distinct <stereoisomers> comprises <enantiomers>; <diastereomers> have no mirror-pair relation.

= Diastereomer
{parent=Stereoisomer}

<Diastereomers> are different <stereoisomers> that are not <enantiomers>. The <meso compound> form of <tartaric acid> is a <diastereomer> of either member of its <enantiomer> pair.

= Absolute configuration
{parent=Stereochemistry}

<Absolute configuration> records a spatial arrangement without leaving its handedness relative to its mirror unspecified. <R and S configuration> is one naming convention; <optical rotation> is a measured response.

= R and S configuration
{c}
{parent=Absolute configuration}
{title2=$R,\ S$}

For a tetrahedral <stereocenter>, rank its four <substituents> using <stereochemical sequence rules>. With lowest-priority group pointing away, a clockwise $1\to2\to3$ sequence is $R$ and a counterclockwise sequence is $S$. Looking from the other side reverses the apparent sequence, so the viewing condition is essential. The geometric letters A/B/C/D in the <tetrahedral mirror-image test> carry no assigned priority. Neither $R$ nor $S$ determines positive or negative <optical rotation>.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/5.5 Sequence Rules for Specifying Configuration>[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §5.5, Figure 5.8 and discussion after Figure 5.10]{ref}

= Stereochemical sequence rule
{parent=Absolute configuration}

<Stereochemical sequence rules> rank <substituents> for <R and S configuration>. Compare the directly attached <chemical atoms> by <atomic number> first, then resolve ties by comparing successive shells at the first difference. <Isotopes> and multiple <chemical bonds> require additional rules. This ranking convention does not involve <optical rotation>.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/5.5 Sequence Rules for Specifying Configuration>[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §5.5, Rules 1–3]{ref}

= Conformational isomerism
{parent=Stereochemistry}

<Conformational isomerism> concerns spatial arrangements interconverted by internal motion, such as turning about a single <chemical bond>. Moving the entire <molecule> or applying a <rotation (mathematics)> changes its position or viewing direction, not its internal arrangement.

= Substituent
{parent=Stereochemistry}

A <substituent> is an attached <chemical atom> or group considered relative to a chosen molecular framework. Testing whether two <substituents> are identical may require comparing their full <chemical bond> connectivity and <stereochemistry>.

= Stereocenter
{parent=Stereochemistry}
{wiki}

At a <stereocenter>, exchanging two attached groups produces a different spatial configuration. The open-chain <ribose> and <glucose> examples have <carbon> <stereocenters> bearing four different groups; C2 of <deoxyribose> has two identical <hydrogen> substituents and is not a <stereocenter>. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/stereo/RS.html#stereogenic[Moss, Basic Terminology of Stereochemistry, IUPAC Recommendations 1996: stereogenic units].

= Fischer projection
{c}
{parent=Stereochemistry}
{wiki}

In a <Fischer projection>, each cross represents a <carbon> position: horizontal <chemical bonds> point toward the viewer and vertical <chemical bonds> point away. For the open-chain <aldehyde> <sugars> illustrated here, place $\mathrm{CHO}$ at the top and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ at the bottom. This is a convention for <stereochemistry>, not a measured molecular shape. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/stereo/FM.html#Fischer[Moss, IUPAC Recommendations 1996: Fischer projection].

In these <Fischer projections>, D specifies that the <hydroxy group> at the lowest <stereocenter> lies on the right: C4 for <ribose> and <deoxyribose>, C5 for <glucose>. D does not specify every other <stereocenter>, and it does not specify the direction of rotation of polarized <light>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-3-d-l-sugars[McMurry: the meaning of D and L].

= Functional group
{parent=Chemistry}

A <functional group> is an identifiable group of <chemical atoms> associated with characteristic <chemical reactions>. In <step-growth polymerization>, $A$ and $B$ name compatible reactive groups, not necessarily entire <monomers> or particular <chemical elements>.

= Hydroxy group
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{OH}$}

= Hydroxyl group
{synonym}

A <hydroxy group> is an $\mathrm{OH}$ group attached to the rest of a <molecule>. In the illustrated <sugars>, its <oxygen> is bonded to <carbon> and to <hydrogen>.

\Include[chemical-substance]{parent=Chemistry}

= Steady state
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A <steady state (chemistry)> has time-independent concentrations because formation and removal rates balance for the species considered. Nonzero reaction fluxes can continue, so a steady state need not be <chemical equilibrium>. A <quasi-steady-state approximation> imposes this balance approximately for a fast intermediate while slower species evolve.

= Physical chemistry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Crystal structure
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{tag=Solid}
{wiki}

= Adsorption
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

<Adsorption> is accumulation of a substance at an interface. Binding a <solute> to a packing surface can delay its passage through a <chromatography> column. Ideal <size-exclusion chromatography> separates through <SEC pore access> without such binding; extra retention from <adsorption> requires a different interpretation.

= Chemical polarity
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

<Chemical polarity> concerns unequal distribution of <electron density> in a <chemical bond> or <molecule>. A <polar molecule> has a permanent <electric dipole moment>; the effects of individual bonds must be combined with their spatial arrangement. In a symmetric linear <carbon dioxide> <molecule>, opposing bond-dipole contributions cancel. In <water>, its <bent molecular geometry> prevents that cancellation, giving a permanent <electric dipole moment>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others>[Chemistry 2e, 7.6: geometry and molecular polarity]{ref}

= Polar molecule
{parent=Chemical polarity}

A <polar molecule> has a nonzero permanent <electric dipole moment> because of its internal <electric charge> distribution. It can still have zero total <electric charge>. <Water> is polar; a <nonpolar molecule> has no permanent <electric dipole moment>.

= Nonpolar molecule
{parent=Chemical polarity}

= Nonpolar
{synonym}

A <nonpolar molecule> has no permanent <electric dipole moment>. This distinguishes its charge distribution from the separated partial charges of a polar <molecule>.

= Hydrophilicity
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

= Hydrophilic
{synonym}

<Hydrophilicity> describes affinity for <water>. Regions with <chemical polarity> or <electric charge> can interact favorably with <water>, including through <hydrogen bonds> where suitable groups are present. <Hydrophilic> regions of membrane-forming <phospholipids> face the <water> on either side of a <lipid bilayer>. Such affinity does not mean forming <covalent bonds> with every neighboring substance, nor does it require a net negative <electric charge>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Alberts et al.: the chemical components of a cell].

= Amphiphile
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

= Amphiphilic
{synonym}

= Amphipathic
{synonym}

An <amphiphile> is a <molecule> containing distinct <hydrophilic> and <hydrophobic> regions. <Amphiphilic> and <amphipathic> describe this combination. Membrane-forming <phospholipids> are examples; their two regions give different parts of one <molecule> different affinities for <water>. The word describes a structure, not a guarantee of a particular aggregate shape. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00303[IUPAC Gold Book: amphiphilic].

= Hydrophobicity
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

= Hydrophobic
{synonym}

<Hydrophobicity> describes unfavorable mixing with <water>, as illustrated by long <hydrocarbon> regions. The <hydrophobic> regions of membrane-forming <phospholipids> face the interior of a <lipid bilayer>. This organization is explained by the <hydrophobic effect>, rather than by imagining that chains actively repel <water>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/H02907[IUPAC Gold Book: hydrophobic interaction].

= Hydrophobic effect
{parent=Hydrophobicity}
{wiki}

The <hydrophobic effect> is the solvent-mediated tendency of <nonpolar> regions to associate in <water>. Exposing a <hydrocarbon> region changes how surrounding <water> <molecules> can arrange and interact; reducing that exposure can lower the total <free energy>. Both <water> and <lipids> enter the accounting. The effect is not an additional <covalent bond> between chains. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/H02907[IUPAC Gold Book: hydrophobic interaction].

At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, compare two candidate organizations by the change in <Gibbs free energy>, $\Delta G=\Delta H-T\Delta S$, where $H$ is <enthalpy>, $T$ is absolute <temperature>, and $S$ is <entropy>. An organization with lower $G$ is favored in this comparison. Reducing exposed <hydrocarbon> area is one contribution; changes in chain packing and the <entropy> of the <lipids> also matter. This explains why <hydrophobicity> alone cannot predict every aggregate shape. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/16.4 Free Energy>.

= Absorbance
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{title2=$A$}
{wiki}

<Absorbance> is the <dimensionless> quantity $A=\log_{10}(P_0/P)$, where $P_0$ and $P$ are reference and transmitted radiant <powers> at the chosen <wavelength>. For example, $A=1$ means one tenth of the reference <power> is transmitted. Under the <Beer-Lambert law>, <absorbance> can determine an absorbing species' <concentration>; attenuation by cloudiness or unrelated colored <solutes> needs separate controls. https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/B00626[IUPAC Gold Book: absorbance and its concentration relation]{ref}

= Beer-Lambert law
{c}
{parent=Absorbance}
{title2=$A=\varepsilon lc$}
{wiki=Beer–Lambert_law}

The <Beer-Lambert law> relates <absorbance> $A$ to an absorbing species' <concentration> $c$ and path length $l$ by $A=\varepsilon lc$ in a homogeneous medium using sufficiently narrow-band light. The coefficient $\varepsilon$ depends on the species, <wavelength> and <chemical solution> conditions. This makes <Ellman's reagent> useful for quantitative <thiol> assays once its released colored species has been calibrated. https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/B00626[IUPAC Gold Book: Beer-Lambert law and validity conditions]{ref}

= Thermochemistry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Hess's law
{c}
{parent=Thermochemistry}
{wiki}

<Hess's law> states that compatible <chemical reaction> <enthalpies> add when their equations add. Reversing a <chemical reaction> reverses its <enthalpy> change; multiplying the entire <chemical reaction> equation by a factor multiplies that change by the same factor. The reason is that <enthalpy> is a <state function>: intermediate states cancel from the differences. A cycle supplies one independent balance relation; it cannot determine two unknown inputs without further information.

= Standard enthalpy of formation
{parent=Thermochemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Delta H_{\rm f}^{\circ}$}

The <standard enthalpy of formation> is the <enthalpy> change for forming one <mole> of a specified <chemical substance> from its <chemical elements> in their reference states at the stated <temperature>. For <sodium chloride> at $298.15\,\mathrm K$, those reactant states are <solid> <sodium> and diatomic gaseous <chlorine>. <Standard state> specifies conditions; it does not by itself select a <temperature>.

= Enthalpy of atomization
{parent=Thermochemistry}
{title2=$\Delta H_{\rm at}^{\circ}$}

An <enthalpy of atomization> is the <enthalpy> change for making one <mole> of separated gaseous <chemical atoms> from a specified starting <chemical substance>. For <sodium>, the relevant step is <sublimation> to monatomic <gas>; for diatomic <chlorine>, it is half the compatible molar <bond-dissociation energy> expressed as an <enthalpy>.

= Enthalpy change of solution
{parent=Thermochemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Delta H_{\rm sol}$}

= Enthalpy of solution
{synonym}

An <enthalpy of solution> is an <enthalpy> difference for dissolution into a specified <solvent> at stated <temperature> and final <concentration>. Keep a measured finite-dilution value distinct from an infinite-dilution reference value.

= Mass spectrometry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

<Mass spectrometry> separates and detects <ions> according to <mass-to-charge ratio>. It can distinguish <isotopes> which belong to the same <chemical element> but have different <atomic masses>. The instrument measures <ions>: interpreting the result as a neutral <atomic mass> requires accounting for <electron> removal and its <ionization energy>. For a singly positive <ion> in its <ground state>, $m_+=m_{\rm a}-m_e+I/c^2$, where $m_e$ is the <electron> <rest mass>, $I$ is the <ionization energy>, and $c$ is the <speed of light>.

For a simple illustrative analyzer, positive <ions> of <mass> $m$ and <electric charge> $q=ze$ gain <kinetic energy> $qV=mv^2/2$ across an <electric potential difference> $V$ before entering a transverse uniform <magnetic field> $B$. The <Lorentz force> supplies <centripetal acceleration>, so
$$
qvB=\frac{mv^2}{r},\qquad\frac{m}{q}=\frac{B^2r^2}{2V}.
$$
At fixed $V$ and $B$, the measured bending radius $r$ separates <mass-to-charge ratios>, not <masses> independently of <electric charge>. This ideal model assumes negligible initial <kinetic energy> and speeds small compared with the <speed of light>; the historical focusing <mass spectrograph> used the geometry described below.

= Molecular ion
{parent=Mass spectrometry}

In the <International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry> (IUPAC) <mass spectrometry> convention, a <molecular ion> is formed by removing or adding one or more <electrons> to a <molecule> without fragmentation of its molecular structure.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/molecular ion in mass spectrometry>[Gold Book, fifth edition, molecular ion in mass spectrometry: formation by electron removal or addition without fragmentation]{ref} For example, removing one <electron> from intact <water> gives $\mathrm{H_2O^{\bullet+}}$: two <hydrogen> and one <oxygen> <chemical atom>, total <electric charge> $+e$, and an unpaired <electron>, hence a <radical cation>. Atom inventory, net <electric charge>, and electronic state are separate pieces of information. <Hydronium>, $\mathrm{H_3O^+}$, has a different inventory; it is a <polyatomic ion>, rather than the <molecular ion> obtained by electron removal from <water>.

= Mass spectrograph
{parent=Mass spectrometry}

A <mass spectrometry> instrument with a photographic readout. In the 1919 apparatus described in <Isotopes by Francis William Aston/Chapter V: The Mass-Spectrograph>, a <gas> discharge produced positive <ions>. Two narrow slits formed a ribbon beam in a <vacuum>; plates applied an <electric field> to spread it, an aperture selected part of it, and a <magnetic field> bent it oppositely. Focusing reduced the spread caused by differing <ion> <speeds>, concentrating a given <mass-to-charge ratio> into a line on a photographic plate.

The observed quantity was line displacement from a fiducial spot. Known reference lines calibrated displacement against <mass-to-charge ratio>; repeated exposures with changed <magnetic field> tested the calibration. Evacuation limited collisions. Comparing line shapes with narrow and wide apertures helped distinguish geometric broadening from photographic spreading. Finite slit widths, plate response, and overlapping molecular or multiply charged <ions> limited interpretation. See <Isotopes by Francis William Aston/Chapter V: The Mass-Spectrograph> (§§33–40: apparatus and photographic procedure; §§43 and 45–46: calibration, aperture comparisons, and accuracy).

In <Mass spectra and isotopes by Francis William Aston> (Nobel lecture, 12 December 1922), the reported <neon> lines at 20 and 22 have companions at 10 and 11 from doubly charged <ions>. That paired pattern and comparison with reference lines support different <atomic masses> within one <chemical element>, rather than an <atomic mass> concentrated at the bulk average. The lecture also describes an earlier failed separation by distillation and partial separation by <diffusion>, showing why the focused <mass spectrum> supplied stronger evidence. These numbers used a historical <oxygen>-based reference scale. The lecture's whole-number rule was an empirical approximation at its stated accuracy, not the modern exact definition $A=Z+N$.<Mass spectra and isotopes by Francis William Aston>[pp. 9–13: separation attempts, focusing, reference lines, charge orders, and neon; pp. 15–17: whole-number rule and historical nuclear interpretation]{ref}

= Mass-to-charge ratio
{parent=Mass spectrometry}
{title2=$m/q$}
{wiki}

The ratio of an <ion>'s <mass> to its <electric charge>; in <SI> it has units $\mathrm{kg/C}$. A convenient numerical <mass spectrum> coordinate is $x=(m/\mathrm u)/z$, with $q=ze$ and positive integer $z$ here. This $x$ is <dimensionless>. Doubling $z$ at fixed <mass> halves $x$, so the <electric charge> state must be identified before assigning an <isotope>.

= Mass spectrum
{parent=Mass spectrometry}
{wiki}

= Mass spectra
{synonym}

A <mass spectrum> displays detected signal against <mass-to-charge ratio>. Peak positions identify candidate <ions>; peak areas give <isotopic abundances> only after the response and overlapping contributions have been accounted for.

\Image[neon-synthetic-mass-spectrum-weighted-mean.png]
{title=Synthetic <neon> <mass spectra> and the <weighted mean> of neutral <atomic masses>}
{description=This is a model, not a measured <mass spectrum> or a reconstruction of Aston's plate. All <ions> have $z=+1$; neutral <atomic masses> approximate <ion> <masses>. Chosen <Gaussian functions> have unit area and widths $\sigma=0.04$ and $0.70$ in the <dimensionless> coordinate $x$. Equal responses make component areas equal the displayed reference <isotopic abundances>. The inset reveals the small <neon-21> peak. The bottom diagram places the <weighted mean> between the component <atomic masses>; it is not an additional <isotope>.}

Broadening can hide a weak component without changing its area in this ideal model. An actual <mass spectrum> can also contain molecular <ions>, different <electric charge> states, background, and unequal detection responses. A missing visible peak therefore cannot by itself prove the absence of an <isotope>. Conversely, a fractional <atomic weight> is a property of a population: there is no requirement for an <ion> peak at the <weighted mean>.

= Mass resolution
{parent=Mass spectrometry}
{title2=$R=x/\Delta x$}
{wiki}

= Mass resolving power
{synonym}

<Mass resolving power> compares a peak coordinate $x$ with a stated width or separation criterion $\Delta x$. With $\Delta x$ taken as <full width at half maximum>, a <Gaussian function> of <standard deviation> $\sigma$ has
$$
\Delta x=2\sqrt{2\log2}\,\sigma,\qquad R=\frac{x}{\Delta x}.
$$
At the <neon-20> coordinate in the synthetic <mass spectrum>, $\sigma=0.04$ gives $\Delta x\approx0.0942$ and $R\approx212$, while $\sigma=0.70$ gives $\Delta x\approx1.648$ and $R\approx12.1$. The width criterion and relative peak areas both matter when judging whether a small neighboring <isotope> can be distinguished. <Mass resolution> also differs from the accuracy of a calibrated peak position.

